window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Axioms, inference rules, and proofs", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-1", "text": "1.1.1 Axioms, inference rules, and proofs § A proof starts from accepted assumptions and reaches a conclusion through allowed steps. The starting assumptions are called axioms . The allowed steps are specified by rules of inference . For example, if we know \\(P\\) and know that \\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\), we may conclude \\(Q\\). In the even-integer example from the introduction, each step used a definition or an arithmetic rule. If we record these justifications explicitly, another person can check the argument without having to discover it again. Now suppose the statements and rules are written precisely enough for each step to be checked by following fixed instructions. A machine could then check a supplied proof. This is the connection we need: reasoning can be represented as a finite sequence of operations. Each arrow shows a dependency between proof steps. The same inference rule first gives \\(Q\\) from \\(P\\), then \\(R\\) from \\(Q\\). Problems What is an axiom, and what is a rule of inference? Given \\(P\\) and “\\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\),” write the conclusion and identify the rule used. If a machine accepts every step of a proof, what has it established about the conclusion—and what has it left unsettled about the axioms? Where does the responsibility for choosing those axioms remain?"}, {"title": "Hilbert’s program and consistency", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-2", "text": "1.1.2 Hilbert’s program and consistency § Writing rules explicitly lets us check whether an argument follows them. But are the rules themselves reliable? Could they allow us to prove both a statement and its opposite? A system that never does this is called consistent . Hilbert wanted to put mathematics on a secure foundation. First, its assumptions and proof rules would be made explicit. Then one would show that those rules could never produce a contradiction. He hoped to give this justification using reasoning about finite objects and concrete operations on them. These were called finitary methods . Even when a mathematical statement concerns infinitely many numbers, its written proof is finite. So, proofs and the rules for constructing them could themselves be studied. This was a question about the reliability of mathematical reasoning. A related question concerns its automation: once the rules are explicit, can a procedure always determine whether a statement has a proof? To see the difficulty, we must distinguish checking a proof from searching for one. Hilbert’s aim was to study the finite proofs allowed by a system and show that none could lead to a contradiction. Problems What does it mean for a formal system to be consistent? A system proves \\(P\\) and also proves “not \\(P\\).” Which requirement of Hilbert’s program has failed? Suppose we justify one system of rules by reasoning in another. What must we then trust about the second system? Why did Hilbert seek a particularly restricted form of reasoning for this justification?"}, {"title": "Proof checking, proof search, and decision", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-3", "text": "1.1.3 Proof checking, proof search, and decision § Suppose we are given a proposed proof. We can check its steps and accept or reject that particular argument. Rejection does not show that the conclusion has no proof; a different argument might work. What if no proof is supplied? Suppose proofs use a fixed, finite collection of symbols. We can list candidate strings in order of length and check each one. Most will not be proofs. But every finite proof eventually appears, so this search finds a proof whenever one exists. What happens if there is no proof? The search keeps trying longer candidates. It never reaches a last candidate that certifies failure. A decision procedure must do more. For every statement we give it, it must finish with a correct answer: “a proof exists” or “no proof exists.” Our search method does not meet that requirement. Could another method succeed? Failure of one search strategy does not show that every possible strategy fails. To investigate that question, we need to specify what counts as a mechanical procedure. This leads us to models of computation . Checking tests one proposed proof. Searching tries candidates until it finds a proof. Deciding must also finish when no proof exists. Problems Does checking a supplied proof require finding that proof first? A checker rejects one proposed proof. Explain why this does not show that the statement has no proof. Imagine searching simultaneously for a proof of a statement and a proof of its negation. Under what assumptions would this become a decision procedure? What happens if neither proof exists?"}, {"title": "Algorithms and termination", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-1", "text": "1.2.1 Algorithms and termination § Let us first consider a procedure whose steps we can examine directly. We want the greatest common divisor of two positive integers: the largest positive integer that divides both without remainder. Euclid’s algorithm repeatedly divides and keeps the remainder. For \\(48\\) and \\(18\\), we obtain \\[48=2\\cdot18+12,\\qquad18=1\\cdot12+6,\\qquad12=2\\cdot6+0.\\] The pairs change as follows: \\[(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12)\\longrightarrow(12,6)\\longrightarrow(6,0).\\] At a step with pair \\((a,b)\\), suppose \\(b\\) is positive. Divide \\(a\\) by \\(b\\). Write the result as \\(a=qb+r\\), where \\(q\\) is the quotient and \\(r\\) is the remainder. The remainder satisfies \\(0\\le r\\lt b\\). Replace \\((a,b)\\) by \\((b,r)\\) and repeat. When the second entry becomes zero, return the first. In our example, we reach \\((6,0)\\), so the answer is six. Why does this give the correct answer? Suppose a number divides both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). It must also divide \\(a-qb\\), which is the remainder \\(r\\). So, every common divisor of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is a common divisor of \\(b\\) and \\(r\\). The reverse also holds. A number dividing both \\(b\\) and \\(r\\) divides \\(qb+r=a\\). Thus the old pair and the new pair have exactly the same common divisors. In particular, their greatest common divisor is unchanged. A property that remains unchanged at every step is called an invariant . Why does the procedure finish? Each new second entry is smaller than the previous one, and it is never negative. A sequence of nonnegative integers cannot keep decreasing forever. Eventually the second entry is zero. At that point the pair is \\((g,0)\\). Its greatest common divisor is \\(g\\): every divisor of \\(g\\) also divides zero. Since the greatest common divisor has stayed unchanged, \\(g\\) is the answer for the original pair. We have established two things: the steps preserve the answer, and the process reaches its stopping condition. These require separate arguments. A procedure that repeats the same pair forever preserves the greatest common divisor but never returns it. So, preserving the answer alone is not enough. The greatest common divisor remains six throughout this run. The second entry decreases through nonnegative integers until it is zero. These are separate reasons for correctness and termination. Problems When does Euclid’s algorithm stop? Starting from (18,12), write the next two pairs and give the greatest common divisor. Euclid’s algorithm preserves the answer while decreasing the second entry. Why are both facts needed? Can you describe a procedure that preserves the right answer at every step but never reaches it?"}, {"title": "Configurations and transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-2", "text": "1.2.2 Configurations and transitions § Suppose we pause Euclid’s algorithm at the pair \\((18,12)\\), just before the next division. What must we record in order to continue? We need the pair and the instruction that comes next. We do not need the earlier pairs. The information needed to resume a computation is called its configuration . What must we include? That depends on where we allow the computation to pause. At the start of Euclid’s repeated step, we need only the pair: the next instruction is already fixed. Halfway through a division, the pair alone would not be enough. We would also need the temporary values and a record of how far the division had progressed. A transition is one step from a configuration to the next. Starting with a particular input, we obtain a sequence of configurations. This sequence is called a run . The algorithm supplies the transition rules and tells us when to stop. Proof search can be described in the same way. We record the candidate being checked, the line currently under examination, and any other information needed by the checker. Each operation updates this configuration. For a deterministic procedure, the current configuration determines the next step. Does it also determine the previous step? In Euclid’s algorithm, both \\((30,18)\\) and \\((48,18)\\) lead to \\((18,12)\\). So, knowing how to continue does not necessarily tell us how to go backward. We will return to this distinction when we consider reversibility. A configuration includes enough information to resume the computation. A transition updates that information; a run is a sequence of these updates. Problems Name the three parts of a Turing-machine configuration. Two machines have the same tape and control state but different head positions. Explain why their next steps could differ. Two executions have the same control state but different tape contents. Must their futures agree? What information must a configuration include before it really determines the next step?"}, {"title": "Turing machines and universal computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-3", "text": "1.2.3 Turing machines and universal computation § We have described computation as a sequence of transitions. But an instruction such as “divide” still contains many operations. To study computation in general, we need a model with explicitly specified elementary steps. A Turing machine has a tape divided into cells. Each cell holds a symbol from a fixed, finite collection, including a blank symbol. A head reads one cell at a time. The machine also has an internal state chosen from a finite list. The state records which stage of its instructions it is in. A rule table uses two pieces of information: the current state and the symbol under the head. For each relevant pair, the rule tells the machine what to write, whether to move one cell left or right, and which state to enter next. If it enters the halting state, the computation stops. For example, place a finite block of \\(1\\) symbols on an otherwise blank tape, with the head at the left end. Give the machine the rule to move right whenever it reads \\(1\\), and to halt when it reads a blank. The same finite instructions work for a block of any finite length. To resume this machine, we need the tape contents, the head position, and the internal state. Together, these make up its configuration. The model does not fix a maximum tape length in advance. More cells are available when needed. This does not mean that a completed calculation uses infinite memory: after finitely many steps, the head has visited only finitely many cells. With more rules, the machine can carry out arithmetic or check proofs. Its own rule table is also a finite object, so we can encode that table as a string of symbols. Another machine can read this description and imitate the described machine, one step at a time. A universal Turing machine can do this for any correctly encoded machine and input. Changing the description changes the computation it simulates. Now a program can be treated as data. We can supply its description to another program, or even supply it to itself. This requires only copying a finite string. It will be useful when we ask what programs can determine about other programs. This gives a general model of computation. We can use it to describe particular algorithms and to formulate questions about all machines in the model. The highlighted cell is under the head. In this example, the machine moves right over each 1 and halts at a blank. A universal machine uses encoded instructions to simulate another machine; it need not halt if the simulated run does not halt. Problems What does a universal Turing machine simulate? To simulate a particular program on a particular input, what two pieces of information must the universal machine receive? A universal machine treats another machine’s instructions as input. What does this change about the boundary between a program and its data? Does the ability to simulate every machine imply the ability to predict which simulations finish?"}, {"title": "The Church–Turing thesis", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-1", "text": "1.3.1 The Church–Turing thesis § We have defined a model using a tape, a head, and a finite rule table. Why use this model to discuss the limits of effective calculation? Could another reasonable model compute functions that Turing machines cannot? Other mathematical models describe computation in different ways. Lambda calculus uses rules for substituting expressions. Recursive-function models build functions from specified basic functions and construction rules. Despite these differences, they compute the same functions as Turing machines. A function computable in one model is computable in the others. This equivalence is a mathematical result that can be proved. The Church–Turing thesis makes a further claim: any calculation that can be carried out by an effective mechanical method can be carried out by a Turing machine. The phrase “effective mechanical method” is an informal idea. A Turing machine is a precisely defined object. The thesis connects the two. The agreement between different formal models supports it, but does not turn it into a theorem about an already defined class of every possible mechanical method. This distinction matters when we establish a limitation. We can prove that no Turing machine solves a specified problem. Applying that result to effective procedures in general uses the thesis. The thesis concerns whether a function can be computed at all. It does not say how much time or memory the computation needs. This matters for quantum computing. In the standard quantum model, the allowed operations must themselves have effective mathematical descriptions. Under this condition, quantum computers do not compute functions that are uncomputable by Turing machines. They can change the resources needed for some tasks that are already computable. We will return to this difference when studying quantum algorithms. The solid arrows connect formal models that can simulate one another. They compute the same functions, though they may take different amounts of time. The dashed arrow marks the further claim of the Church–Turing thesis: these models capture what we mean informally by an effective method. Problems Which informal idea does the Church–Turing thesis connect to Turing machines? A new programming language can be simulated by a Turing machine. Does this establish that it computes more functions than a Turing machine? Explain. Why does agreement between several formal models support the Church–Turing thesis without proving it in the same way as a theorem about those models? What would a proposed counterexample have to explain?"}, {"title": "The halting problem", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-2", "text": "1.3.2 The halting problem § Given a program and its input, can we always determine whether the computation will stop? We know that Euclid’s algorithm stops for its permitted inputs. We also know that a program instructed to repeat forever does not stop. Can one procedure settle this question for every program and input? Suppose such a procedure exists. Call it \\(H(P,x)\\), where \\(P\\) is a program description and \\(x\\) is its input. We require \\(H\\) itself to finish and answer correctly on every valid pair. Let us use \\(H\\) to construct another program, \\(D\\). Its input \\(p\\) is a program description. First, \\(D\\) calls \\(H(p,p)\\): it asks what happens when the program described by \\(p\\) receives its own description as input. Then \\(D\\) takes the opposite action. If \\(H\\) answers “halts,” \\(D\\) enters an endless loop. If \\(H\\) answers “does not halt,” \\(D\\) stops. If the input is not a valid program description, we can simply let \\(D\\) stop. Now let \\(d\\) be the description of \\(D\\), and give \\(d\\) to \\(D\\). What should \\(H(d,d)\\) answer? If it answers “halts,” the instructions of \\(D\\) make this run continue forever. If it answers “does not halt,” the instructions make the run stop. In either case, the prediction is wrong. We assumed that \\(H\\) was an algorithm that always finished and answered correctly. That assumption allowed us to use it as part of \\(D\\). But the resulting program contradicts the assumption. Therefore no such general \\(H\\) exists. This result is called the undecidability of the halting problem . This does not prevent us from determining the behavior of particular programs. We already proved that Euclid’s algorithm terminates. It also permits an analyzer that sometimes answers “unknown.” What is excluded is one algorithm that always finishes with a correct definite answer for every case. Assume \\(H\\) always finishes and predicts correctly. Program \\(D\\) follows its prediction and takes the opposite action. Now give \\(D\\) its own description \\(d\\). \\(H(d,d)\\) is predicting this very run, so either answer is contradicted by what \\(D\\) does. Problems What two possible answers must a halting decider give? A program immediately stops. Another repeats an instruction forever. Can you decide these two cases without solving the general halting problem? The diagonal program behaves against the halting predictor when given its own description. Why does this produce a contradiction for a supposedly universal predictor, but not for a tool that correctly analyzes only some programs?"}, {"title": "Undecidability and incompleteness", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-3", "text": "1.3.3 Undecidability and incompleteness § We began by asking whether explicit rules could make mathematical reasoning reliable and whether a procedure could settle every question about proofs. The halting result shows how to prove a limitation on procedures. We now briefly consider the corresponding limits on what a system of axioms can prove. A problem is undecidable if no algorithm always finishes with the correct yes-or-no answer for every input. A theory is incomplete if some sentence can neither be proved nor refuted using its axioms and rules. The first is a limitation on procedures; the second is a limitation on what follows from chosen assumptions. To state the Gödel–Rosser incompleteness theorem, first consider a theory with three properties. It is consistent. Its axioms can be listed by an algorithm. And it contains enough arithmetic to express basic calculations with nonnegative integers. For a concrete example of the last requirement, take Peano arithmetic. This is the usual first-order theory of nonnegative integers with addition, multiplication, and induction. The theorem applies to consistent theories that include these axioms and whose axioms can be listed algorithmically. It also applies to certain weaker arithmetic theories. The conclusion is that some sentence \\(P\\) has neither a proof nor a refutation in the theory. The theory proves neither \\(P\\) nor \\(\\neg P\\). A theory whose axioms can be listed by an algorithm is called effectively axiomatized . The list may be infinite: each axiom only needs to appear after finitely many steps. A proof can include a record showing when a cited axiom appears on that list. This lets a checker verify the use of that axiom in finite time. Can we settle an unanswered sentence by adding it as a new axiom? We can. But if the enlarged theory still meets the theorem’s conditions, some sentence will again remain unsettled. It need not be the same sentence. The result does not apply to every formal theory; the conditions matter. Gödel’s second incompleteness theorem concerns the consistency question. A theory with enough arithmetic can write statements about its own encoded proofs. In particular, it can express the claim that none of those proofs derives a contradiction. For theories satisfying the theorem’s arithmetic and proof-representation conditions, the result is this: if the theory is consistent, it cannot prove that standard statement of its own consistency. So, a consistency argument whose steps can all be carried out within the same theory cannot provide this justification. A stronger theory may prove that a weaker one is consistent. But we must then examine the assumptions of the stronger theory. So, formal reasoning has precise limitations. We can still prove particular statements and construct algorithms for particular tasks. For the tasks that admit algorithms, we can now ask how those algorithms should be implemented. On the left, we ask whether an algorithm can decide a problem for every input. On the right, we ask what a particular theory can prove. The incompleteness result requires the stated conditions on that theory. An unsuccessful search alone establishes neither result. Problems Does “unprovable in this theory” mean “disproved in this theory”? A sentence has neither a proof nor a disproof in a theory. State what would change if it were added as an axiom. If a sentence is undecidable in one formal theory, could adding it as an axiom settle it there? Why would that not establish a general algorithm for deciding every sentence?"}, {"title": "Deterministic and reversible transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-1", "text": "1.4.1 Deterministic and reversible transitions § In a deterministic computation, each configuration has at most one next configuration. A halting configuration may have none. When the computation can continue, the current configuration fixes what happens next. Does knowing the later configuration also tell us which earlier one produced it? Determinism does not require this. For example, Euclid’s algorithm includes the transitions \\[(30,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12),\\qquad(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12).\\] Knowing the pair \\((18,12)\\) lets us continue the calculation, but it does not identify which earlier pair occurred. Different configurations have merged into the same one. To undo a step, we must be able to recover the earlier configuration from the full resulting configuration. This requires different inputs to have different outputs. Such a transition is called one-to-one . Logical reversibility requires this extra condition. Suppose the transition also reaches every configuration in a fixed state space. It is then a bijection : each configuration has exactly one predecessor. We can define the reverse transformation everywhere on that space. So, knowing what happens next does not necessarily tell us what happened before. We now ask whether useful computations can preserve enough information to be reversed. On the left, each input has a unique successor, but \\(C\\) has two possible predecessors. On the right, each output identifies one predecessor. Reversibility imposes a condition on the backward direction as well. Problems What does a deterministic transition rule specify? Draw \\(A\\to C\\) and \\(B\\to C\\). Is this rule deterministic on \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)? Can \\(C\\) identify its predecessor? A deterministic rule fixes one future for each present state. Why need it not fix one past? Draw a transition pattern that separates these two requirements."}, {"title": "Information loss in computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-2", "text": "1.4.2 Information loss in computation § Consider the Boolean AND operation. Its output is one only when both input bits are one. The inputs \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) all produce zero. If we retain only this output, we cannot recover the input pair. Must we discard the input to obtain the answer? Suppose we want to calculate \\(f(x)\\). If we replace \\(x\\) by its answer, two different inputs may become indistinguishable. If we keep both the input and the answer as \\((x,f(x))\\), the input remains available. This suggests using extra storage while carrying out the calculation. Keeping the input is only a starting idea. We must choose elementary steps that can be undone. We must say what values the extra storage holds at the start. We must also decide what to do with temporary results. The next chapter develops these constructions using Boolean functions and circuits. Why does this matter for quantum computing? When a quantum system evolves without interacting with anything outside it, its evolution is described by a unitary transformation . Such a transformation has an inverse: mathematically, it can be undone. This is why reversible operations are important in quantum circuits. We need to learn how to obtain an answer through steps that preserve this possibility of reversal. Measurement and discarding part of a system are different kinds of operations, which we will treat separately. We have moved from asking whether a task admits an algorithm to asking how the algorithm transforms information. The limits of computability do not settle this implementation question. We now need to examine the operations used in the computation itself. On the left, the AND output does not tell us which input produced it. On the right, we keep both input bits and put their AND in an extra target bit, initially zero. The full update uses XOR, or addition modulo two. Applying it twice restores the original three bits. Chapter 2 explains the construction. Problems What information is lost when both 0 and 1 are reset to 0? Compare returning only \\(a+b\\) with returning \\((a,b,a+b)\\). Which output allows you to recover both inputs? If two inputs lead to the same answer, has the computation necessarily erased information? How does your answer depend on whether you inspect only the output or the entire machine state?"}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-1", "text": "A proof search has not found a proof after one million candidates. Does this establish unprovability? No. It establishes only that the checked records were not proofs of the sentence. An independent argument would be needed to establish unprovability."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-2", "text": "Run Euclid’s algorithm on (84, 30). Explain separately why the answer is correct and why the run stops. The pairs are \\((84,30)\\to(30,24)\\to(24,6)\\to(6,0)\\), so the answer is 6. Replacing \\((a,b)\\) with \\((b,a-qb)\\) preserves common divisors. The nonnegative second entry strictly decreases until it reaches zero. These justify correctness and termination separately."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-3", "text": "Give two distinct predecessors of (7, 3) under one Euclid step. What information would distinguish them? \\((10,7)\\) and \\((17,7)\\) both become \\((7,3)\\). Retaining the quotient \\(q\\) distinguishes these predecessors: \\(a=7q+3\\). More generally, a reversible implementation must preserve enough information to reconstruct each previous full state."}, {"title": "Binary encodings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-1", "text": "2.1.1 Binary encodings § A computation acts on configurations made of bits. How, then, can it work with numbers, text, or graphs? We choose an encoding : a rule that writes each object as a string of bits. An integer can be written in binary, text character by character, and a graph as a list of its edges. With two bits, for example, the integers \\(0,1,2,3\\) become \\(00,01,10,11\\). The bits themselves carry no meaning. The string \\(01000001\\) is the integer \\(65\\) in binary and the letter A in the ASCII character code. Meaning belongs to the encoding. Why does the choice of encoding matter? It fixes the length \\(n\\) of the input, and the cost of a computation will be measured against \\(n\\). With \\(n\\) bits there are \\(2^n\\) possible inputs. For a fixed length \\(n\\), a computation is then a function from \\(n\\)-bit strings to bit strings. A procedure that works for every length is a family of such functions, one for each \\(n\\), built in a systematic way rather than separately for each length. An encoding supplies meaning. The bits alone do not tell a machine whether to treat \\(01000001\\) as a number or a character. Problems How many different strings can two bits represent? Write the integers zero through three using two-bit binary strings. The same bit string can represent an integer, a character, or an instruction. Which part of its meaning belongs to the bits, and which part belongs to the encoding convention? How could changing the encoding change the cost of a task?"}, {"title": "Boolean functions and truth tables", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-2", "text": "2.1.2 Boolean functions and truth tables § What exactly do we want to compute? Take a yes/no question about an \\(n\\)-bit input. Its answer is a Boolean function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), which assigns \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) to every input. An answer of several bits is a list of such functions, one for each output bit. The most direct way to specify \\(f\\) is to list every input together with its output. This list is the truth table of \\(f\\). For AND and OR on two bits: Input \\(xy\\) AND OR \\(00\\) \\(0\\) \\(0\\) \\(01\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(10\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(11\\) \\(1\\) \\(1\\) Does a truth table tell us how to compute \\(f\\)? It tells us what the answer is, not how to obtain it. It also grows quickly: a function of \\(n\\) bits has \\(2^n\\) rows, so the table alone is exponentially large. We want a description built from a few simple operations. The truth table lists four possible inputs. Only the input \\(11\\) produces one; a circuit must implement all four rows. Problems What values can a Boolean variable take? List all input pairs for a two-input AND gate and give their outputs. A truth table specifies every output of a Boolean function. Why does possessing this complete description not necessarily give an efficient way to compute the function as the number of inputs grows?"}, {"title": "Polynomial representation over the binary field", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-3", "text": "2.1.3 Polynomial representation over the binary field § Can a truth table be turned into a formula? Use arithmetic modulo two, in which \\(1+1=0\\). Addition modulo two is then XOR, and multiplication is AND. The idea is to build, for each row \\(s\\) of the table, a detector that equals \\(1\\) on the input \\(s\\) and \\(0\\) on every other input. The factor \\(x_i\\) equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x_i=1\\), and the factor \\(1+x_i\\) equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x_i=0\\). Multiplying one such factor for each bit gives \\[\\delta_s(x)=\\prod_{i:\\,s_i=1}x_i\\prod_{i:\\,s_i=0}(1+x_i).\\] For two bits \\((x,y)\\), the detector of the row \\(01\\) is \\((1+x)y\\). Now add the detectors of the rows on which \\(f\\) equals \\(1\\): \\[f(x)=\\sum_{s:\\,f(s)=1}\\delta_s(x)\\pmod 2.\\] Why is this \\(f\\)? On any input \\(x\\), exactly one detector, \\(\\delta_x\\), equals \\(1\\). If the row \\(x\\) is among those added, the sum is \\(1\\); otherwise it is \\(0\\). For OR, the rows with output \\(1\\) are \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\): \\[(1+x)y+x(1+y)+xy=x+y+3xy=x+y+xy\\pmod 2.\\] Check the row \\(11\\): \\(1+1+1=1\\) modulo two, as required, whereas ordinary addition would give \\(3\\). Expanding the detectors in this way writes every Boolean function as a polynomial in its input bits. All additions are modulo two. The polynomial \\(x+y+xy\\) reproduces OR on Boolean inputs; its interpretation depends on this arithmetic. Problems What are \\(1+1\\) and \\(1\\times1\\) in arithmetic modulo two? Evaluate \\(x+y+xy\\) modulo two for all four bit pairs. Which Boolean operation does it describe? Why can \\(x^2\\) be replaced by \\(x\\) for Boolean inputs even though these are different polynomials over unrestricted numbers? What does this tell you about the role of the allowed input domain in an algebraic representation?"}, {"title": "Polynomial evaluation by circuits", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-1", "text": "2.2.1 Polynomial evaluation by circuits § How do we evaluate \\(x+y+xy\\) one operation at a time? Compute \\(u=xy\\) with an AND gate, compute \\(x+y\\) with an XOR gate, and combine the two results with a second XOR gate. This arrangement is a circuit . Its gates are elementary operations, and its wires carry bits from one gate to the next; here a wire carries \\(u\\) from the AND gate to the last XOR gate. A wire may also carry a fixed bit, \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). No wire leads back to an earlier gate, so the gates can be evaluated in order. The same method works for every polynomial: multiply factors with AND gates and add terms with XOR gates. So every Boolean function of a fixed number of bits has a circuit. Does it have a small circuit? Not necessarily: the polynomial built from a truth table can have exponentially many terms. That every function can be built is one claim; that it can be built economically is another. To evaluate \\(z=ab+c\\) over bits, compute the product once and pass that bit to XOR. Here \\(+\\) means XOR, so the diagram is also a circuit plan. Problems What does a wire carry between Boolean gates? For \\(z=(a \\text{ AND } b) \\text{ XOR } c\\), identify the intermediate value and draw the two operations in order. Two algebraically equivalent expressions can produce circuits of different sizes. Where can sharing an intermediate result save gates, and what extra information about a computation does a circuit reveal beyond its truth table?"}, {"title": "NAND universality", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-2", "text": "2.2.2 NAND universality § Do we need AND, XOR, and NOT as separate kinds of gate? One kind is enough. NAND outputs the negation of AND, \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)=\\neg(a\\land b)\\); it is \\(0\\) only when \\(a=b=1\\). Tying both inputs to the same bit gives NOT: \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,a)=\\neg a\\). Negating a NAND output gives AND. XOR takes four NAND gates: \\[t=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\quad u=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,t),\\quad v=\\operatorname{NAND}(b,t),\\quad a\\oplus b=\\operatorname{NAND}(u,v).\\] Checking the four input pairs confirms the last identity. Since NAND builds AND, XOR, and NOT, it can evaluate the polynomial of any Boolean function. A gate with this property is universal . In this construction one bit often feeds several gates: \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) each feed two NAND gates, and so does \\(t\\). Copying a bit in this way, called fan-out , is free in classical circuits. Four NAND gates compute XOR. Branch dots indicate shared classical inputs; small output circles indicate negation. Problems When does NAND output zero? Use \\(\\mathrm{NAND}(x,x)\\) to compute the output for \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=1\\). Which operation have you built? NAND can express every Boolean function. Does that make it the best gate for every implementation? Separate the claim that something can be built from the claim that it can be built economically."}, {"title": "Irreversible Boolean operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-3", "text": "2.2.3 Irreversible Boolean operations § Given the output of an AND gate, can we recover its inputs? If the output is \\(1\\), yes: the input was \\(11\\). If it is \\(0\\), the input could have been \\(00\\), \\(01\\), or \\(10\\). Three inputs merge into one output, so the map has no inverse. This is logical information loss , and it happens whether or not the gate computes something useful. Is a circuit irreversible whenever one of its gates is? Not in its overall effect: a circuit can compute a one-to-one function of its input while erasing intermediate values along the way. Its truth table is then reversible , but its individual steps are not. Whether information is lost is a question about each step, not only about the overall truth table. The AND gate maps \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) to the same output. Output zero cannot identify which of these inputs occurred. Problems Can you always recover both AND inputs from its output? List the input pairs that give AND output zero. Use the list to explain the loss of information. An AND output of one identifies its input, while an output of zero does not. Why is the gate still classified as irreversible? Is reversibility a property of one observed run or of the whole allowed map?"}, {"title": "Landauer’s principle", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-1", "text": "2.3.1 Landauer’s principle § Does losing information cost anything physically? A bit is stored in a physical system, such as a capacitor that is charged or uncharged. Resetting an unknown bit to \\(0\\) merges two physical possibilities into one. Landauer’s principle states that erasing a bit that is equally likely to be \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), at temperature \\(T\\) and without keeping a record of its value elsewhere, releases on average at least \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\) of heat into the surroundings. Here \\(k_{\\mathrm B}\\) is Boltzmann’s constant. The conditions matter. A bit already known to be \\(0\\) can be left alone at no cost. A biased bit, or a bit whose value is recorded elsewhere, carries less uncertainty and needs less. The principle is not a fixed charge on every gate; it is a lower bound on the cost of destroying information. The information lost by AND, or by a reset, therefore has a physical counterpart: heat. Further detail: Bennett: Landauer’s principle and reversible computation . Both possible logical inputs are reset to zero. For erasure of one equally likely bit in the standard isothermal setting, the minimum heat delivered to the environment is kBT ln 2. Problems Under the stated conditions, what happens to Landauer’s minimum erasure cost when temperature increases? Compare erasing two independent, equally likely bits with erasing one at the same temperature. How do their minimum costs compare? Erasing a known zero and erasing an unknown bit can leave the same final register value. What differs about the information being discarded? Why must a physical claim about erasure specify the initial situation?"}, {"title": "Logical reversibility and dissipation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-2", "text": "2.3.2 Logical reversibility and dissipation § If a computation never erases information, does it produce no heat? Not necessarily. A logically reversible map, one that is one-to-one on its allowed states, avoids the cost that erasure forces. A real device may still dissipate energy through resistance, friction, imperfect control, or the speed at which it runs. Conversely, can a gate that loses information be made reversible by operating it slowly? No. Running AND slowly does not make its output reveal its input. Reversibility is a property of the map, not of how the map is carried out. Two questions must therefore be kept apart: whether the mathematical map can be inverted, and how much energy its physical realization uses. For quantum computation the first question is decisive, because the operations of an isolated quantum system can always be inverted. A reversible truth table settles whether the input is recoverable. It does not specify the energy dissipated by a particular physical device. Problems Does logical reversibility alone guarantee that a physical device produces no heat? A gate’s truth table is reversible, but its implementation dissipates energy. Explain why these statements do not contradict each other. Could a logically reversible circuit still dissipate substantial energy in its physical implementation? What does knowing its truth table tell you, and what physical details does that table leave out?"}, {"title": "Reversible function embeddings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-3", "text": "2.3.3 Reversible function embeddings § Can every function be computed without erasing information, even one such as AND that merges its inputs? It can, provided we keep the input. Add a target bit \\(t\\) and replace \\(f\\) by the map \\[U_f:(x,t)\\mapsto(x,\\,t\\oplus f(x)).\\] The input \\(x\\) is kept, and \\(f(x)\\) is added to the target modulo two. With \\(t=0\\), the target becomes the answer \\(f(x)\\). Why is this map reversible? Apply it twice: \\(t\\oplus f(x)\\oplus f(x)=t\\), because adding the same bit twice modulo two gives zero. The map is its own inverse. For AND it is \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\): \\((x,y,t)\\) \\((x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\) \\((x,y,t)\\) \\((x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\) \\(000\\) \\(000\\) \\(001\\) \\(001\\) \\(010\\) \\(010\\) \\(011\\) \\(011\\) \\(100\\) \\(100\\) \\(101\\) \\(101\\) \\(110\\) \\(111\\) \\(111\\) \\(110\\) Has AND become invertible? No: \\(f\\) itself is unchanged and still merges its inputs. What is invertible is the larger map, whose output keeps \\(x\\). The extra bits are not mere bookkeeping; they are what makes the transformation one-to-one. For \\(f(x)=1\\), the target flips. Applying the same reversible oracle twice restores the input and target because XOR by the same bit cancels. Problems Which register is preserved in \\((x,t)\\to(x,t \\text{ XOR } f(x))\\)? Set \\(f(0)=1\\) and start with \\((x,t)=(0,0)\\). Apply the update twice. What returns to its original value? A reversible embedding retains the input while updating a target bit. Has it made the original many-to-one function invertible, or changed what counts as the full output? Explain why this distinction matters."}, {"title": "Controlled reversible operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-1", "text": "2.4.1 Controlled reversible operations § How can a gate act on one bit depending on another without losing anything? Keep the deciding bit unchanged, and let it choose a reversible update of the other bit. The kept bit is the control ; the updated bit is the target . The simplest example is controlled-NOT, or CNOT : \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a,b\\oplus a)\\). When the control \\(a\\) is \\(0\\), the target is unchanged; when \\(a\\) is \\(1\\), the target flips. So \\(00\\to00\\), \\(01\\to01\\), \\(10\\to11\\), and \\(11\\to10\\). Applying CNOT twice restores both bits. Why does this work? The control survives the gate, so we know which update was applied, and each update, doing nothing or flipping, is its own inverse. Would it be enough to make the target update reversible but discard the control? No: without the control we would not know whether to flip the target back. CNOT preserves its control bit. For control zero it leaves the target alone; for control one it flips the target. Each branch can be undone. Problems In a controlled-NOT gate, which bit determines whether the target flips? Apply CNOT to \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\), taking the first bit as control. A controlled gate uses one bit to determine what happens to another while preserving the control. Why is preserving the control helpful when reconstructing the past? Would merely knowing that a conditional instruction was executed be enough?"}, {"title": "CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-2", "text": "2.4.2 CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates § Is CNOT enough to build every reversible computation? Each bit produced by CNOT and NOT gates is a sum modulo two of input bits, possibly plus \\(1\\). Such gates can therefore never produce the product \\(xy\\). The Toffoli gate adds a second control: \\((a,b,c)\\mapsto(a,b,c\\oplus ab)\\). With its target starting at \\(0\\), it writes the AND of its controls while keeping them. With a control fixed at \\(1\\) it acts as CNOT, and with both controls fixed at \\(1\\) as NOT. Since AND and NOT suffice for every Boolean function, Toffoli gates, together with extra bits set to fixed values, can compute every Boolean function reversibly. The Fredkin gate keeps one control and swaps two targets when the control is \\(1\\). Toffoli and Fredkin gates are each their own inverse. In circuit drawings, a filled dot marks a control that acts when it is \\(1\\), a circled plus marks a target that is updated by XOR, and a vertical line joins the parts of one gate. CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin preserve their full inputs in reversible form. Each drawing shows one gate, with time running left to right. Problems How many control bits does a Toffoli gate have? Apply Toffoli to \\(110\\) and \\(100\\), with the last bit as target. Explain why only one target flips. CNOT and Toffoli are both reversible, but Toffoli can write the AND of two inputs into a blank target. What additional dependence on the inputs does Toffoli introduce? Why does reversibility alone not determine a gate’s computational usefulness?"}, {"title": "Workspace and uncomputation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-3", "text": "2.4.3 Workspace and uncomputation § Larger computations need intermediate results. Compute the AND of three bits \\(x,y,z\\) with Toffoli gates, using two extra bits that start at \\(0\\): a workspace bit \\(w\\) and an output bit \\(t\\). The first Toffoli gate writes \\(w=xy\\); the second, with controls \\(w\\) and \\(z\\), writes \\(t=xyz\\): \\[(x,y,z,0,0)\\to(x,y,z,xy,0)\\to(x,y,z,xy,xyz).\\] The answer is in \\(t\\), but \\(w\\) still holds \\(xy\\). What should we do with it? Resetting \\(w\\) to \\(0\\) erases it, with the cost of erasure. Leaving it means that the workspace keeps a record of the computation’s history, and every further computation adds more. There is a third option: apply the first Toffoli gate again. It is its own inverse, so it returns \\(w\\) to \\(xy\\oplus xy=0\\): \\[(x,y,z,xy,xyz)\\to(x,y,z,0,xyz).\\] This is uncomputation . In general, a reversible computation of \\(f\\) produces its answer together with intermediate values \\(g(x)\\), often called garbage. Copy the answer into a fresh register with CNOT gates, then run the computation backward: \\[(x,0,0;0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x);0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x);f(x))\\to(x,0,0;f(x)).\\] Only the input and the copied answer remain. Why go to this trouble? Classically, it avoids the cost of erasure. Quantum computation adds a stronger reason: leftover records of a computation’s history interfere with the computation itself. Compute the answer, copy its value to a fresh output, then apply the inverse computation. \\(A\\) may act on a larger workspace than the two schematic wires shown. Problems What is temporary workspace used for? Put these steps in order: reverse the computation, compute the result, copy the basis-state result into a clean output. If a computation retains every intermediate value, it can preserve its history but exhaust its workspace. How does computing, recording the result, and running backward avoid keeping that history forever? What information remains at the end?"}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is AND reversible if only its one-bit output is retained? No. The output does not uniquely identify the input. Keeping the inputs changes the full map and allows a reversible embedding."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-2", "text": "Apply \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t \\text{ XOR } xy)\\) to \\(110\\), \\(111\\), and \\(010\\). Then apply it again. \\(110\\to111\\to110\\); \\(111\\to110\\to111\\); \\(010\\to010\\to010\\). The gate is its own inverse on all three bits, even though AND alone is many-to-one."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-3", "text": "After computing an answer and temporary workspace, why not simply reset the workspace to zero? Describe a reversible alternative for classical basis inputs. Resetting erases the distinctions stored in the workspace. Compute the result, XOR it into a clean output register, then apply the inverse computation to the original registers. This restores the workspace while retaining the input and recorded output. Copying basis labels this way does not clone an arbitrary unknown quantum state."}, {"title": "Deterministic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-1", "text": "3.1.1 Deterministic transitions § Let us begin with the smallest computation we can examine completely: a register holding one bit, updated step by step by a rule. What must we record in order to continue such a computation? Only the value of the bit. The register therefore has two configurations , \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). A transition rule tells us, for each configuration, which configuration comes next. Consider two rules: NOT flips the bit, and RESET writes \\(0\\) whatever the bit holds. Configuration Next under NOT Next under RESET \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(0\\) Start from \\(0\\) and apply NOT twice. The bit goes to \\(1\\), then back to \\(0\\). Such a sequence of configurations is a run , written \\(c_0,c_1,c_2\\), where \\(c_t\\) is the configuration after \\(t\\) steps. Under RESET, the run from \\(0\\) is \\(0,0,0\\), and the run from \\(1\\) is \\(1,0,0\\). A rule is easier to study when we draw it. In a state diagram , each configuration is a circle, and each transition the rule allows is an arrow. We attach a number to each arrow, its weight . For NOT and RESET every weight is \\(1\\): the step certainly follows that arrow. Other kinds of rule will need other weights. A state diagram shows what can happen in one step, but not how a run unfolds in time. For that, draw a copy of the configurations for each time \\(t=0,1,2\\), and connect \\(i\\) at time \\(t\\) to \\(j\\) at time \\(t+1\\) whenever the rule has the arrow \\(i\\to j\\). This is the time-expanded diagram . A run is now a path through it, from time \\(0\\) to time \\(2\\). How many paths leave the start? For NOT, only one, because a single arrow leaves each configuration. A rule with exactly one outgoing arrow at every configuration is deterministic . Can we also run the computation backward? Knowing that NOT ended at \\(0\\), we know it was at \\(1\\) a step earlier, because only one arrow enters \\(0\\). Under RESET we cannot tell: arrows from \\(0\\) and from \\(1\\) both enter \\(0\\). A deterministic rule is reversible when, in addition, exactly one arrow enters each configuration. NOT: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). The only path is \\(0\\to1\\to0\\). Problems In a state diagram, what makes a rule deterministic, and what additionally makes it reversible? Draw the state diagram of RESET. Draw its time-expanded diagram for two steps from \\(1\\). Where does the run end, and can the end reveal the start? Euclid’s algorithm has infinitely many configurations. In what sense does a state diagram still describe it, and how does the merge \\((30,18)\\to(18,12)\\) and \\((48,18)\\to(18,12)\\) appear?"}, {"title": "Nondeterministic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-2", "text": "3.1.2 Nondeterministic transitions § What if a rule allows more than one next configuration? Consider KEEP-OR-FLIP: after either configuration, both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) are allowed. Its state diagram has all four arrows, which we label “yes.” A rule of this kind is nondeterministic . Starting from \\(0\\), how many runs are there? Two arrows leave the start, and two leave each configuration after that, so four paths reach time \\(2\\): \\[0\\to0\\to0,\\qquad0\\to1\\to0,\\qquad0\\to0\\to1,\\qquad0\\to1\\to1.\\] The rule does not say which path is taken. What can we still ask? Whether a run is possible : it is when every arrow on its path is allowed. And whether a configuration is reachable : it is when some possible path ends there. Here both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) are reachable after two steps. Two paths reach \\(0\\). Does that make \\(0\\) twice as likely? No. The rule lists what is allowed and says nothing about likelihood. Nondeterminism is still a useful idea: a nondeterministic machine accepts an input if some possible run accepts it, and Chapter 9 defines the class NP in this way. To speak about how likely an outcome is, the arrows need numbers of another kind. KEEP-OR-FLIP: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). Four paths are possible; both configurations are reachable at time \\(2\\). Problems What does a nondeterministic rule specify, and what does it leave out? A rule allows \\(0\\to0\\), \\(0\\to1\\), and \\(1\\to1\\), but forbids \\(1\\to0\\). List the possible two-step runs from \\(0\\). Which end configurations are reachable? Two possible paths reach \\(0\\) and one reaches \\(1\\). Why does this not make \\(0\\) twice as likely?"}, {"title": "Probabilistic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-3", "text": "3.1.3 Probabilistic transitions § Suppose the rule chooses at random. Each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) then carries a probability \\(P(j\\mid i)\\), read “the probability of \\(j\\) given \\(i\\)”: the probability that the next configuration is \\(j\\) when the current one is \\(i\\). Some next configuration must occur, so the probabilities on the arrows leaving a configuration add to \\(1\\). Such a rule is probabilistic . Our example is COIN: whatever the bit holds, replace it by a fair coin flip. Every arrow carries \\(1/2\\). What is the probability that a run from \\(0\\) ends at \\(0\\)? Take one path, \\(0\\to1\\to0\\). The first arrow is followed with probability \\(1/2\\). Given that it was, the second is followed with probability \\(1/2\\). The path therefore has probability \\(1/2\\cdot1/2=1/4\\). We have used that each step depends only on the current configuration, not on how the run reached it; a process with this property is a Markov process . Two paths end at \\(0\\). A run follows exactly one path, so these alternatives cannot both happen, and their probabilities add: \\[P(c_2=0)=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12.\\] Likewise \\(P(c_2=1)=1/2\\). A fair coin flipped twice is still fair. What does a probability tell us about a single run? Nothing definite: a single run ends at \\(0\\) or at \\(1\\). The probability predicts how often each happens when the run is repeated many times. COIN: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). Each path has probability \\(1/4\\); two paths end at each configuration. Problems What do the labels on a probabilistic state diagram mean, and why must the labels leaving a configuration sum to one? A step keeps the bit with probability \\(3/4\\) and flips it with probability \\(1/4\\). Draw the time-expanded diagram for two steps from \\(0\\) and find the probability of ending at \\(0\\). Show that no probabilistic step can undo COIN."}, {"title": "The product rule along a path", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-1", "text": "3.2.1 The product rule along a path § Look back at the three calculations. Each time we did two things: we combined the arrows along a path, and then we combined the paths ending at the same configuration. Can one recipe cover all three kinds of rule? Give every arrow a weight: \\(1\\) for an arrow of a deterministic or nondeterministic rule, \\(P(j\\mid i)\\) for an arrow of a probabilistic rule, and \\(0\\) for an arrow the rule does not have. Along a path, multiply. Product rule. The weight of a path is the product of the weights of its arrows: \\[w(i\\to j\\to k)=w(j\\to k)\\,w(i\\to j).\\] Does this give the right answer each time? For a deterministic rule, the product is \\(1\\) along the run and \\(0\\) on any path that uses a missing arrow. For a nondeterministic rule, a product of ones and zeros is \\(1\\) exactly when every arrow on the path is allowed. For a probabilistic rule, it is the probability of the path, as we found for COIN. Under COIN, every path of \\(n\\) steps has weight \\((1/2)^n\\). Multiplying the weights along the path \\(0\\to1\\to0\\) gives its weight in each model. Problems State the product rule. Using the step with \\(P(0\\mid0)=3/4\\), \\(P(1\\mid0)=1/4\\), \\(P(0\\mid1)=1/4\\), and \\(P(1\\mid1)=3/4\\), find the probability of the three-step path \\(0\\to1\\to1\\to0\\). Why does the product rule for a nondeterministic rule give the same answer as asking whether every arrow is allowed?"}, {"title": "The sum rule across paths", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-2", "text": "3.2.2 The sum rule across paths § Now combine the paths. Several paths can end at the same configuration, and we add their weights. Sum rule. The weight of reaching \\(k\\) from \\(i\\) is the sum of the weights of all paths from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\). In two steps the paths differ only in the intermediate configuration \\(j\\), so \\[w(i\\Rightarrow k)=\\sum_j w(j\\to k)\\,w(i\\to j),\\] where the sum runs over all configurations \\(j\\). For a probabilistic rule this is the calculation we made for COIN: \\[P(k\\mid i)=\\sum_jP(k\\mid j)\\,P(j\\mid i)=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12.\\] What does the sum mean for the other kinds of rule? For a deterministic rule, at most one path has weight \\(1\\), so the sum is \\(1\\) if the run ends at \\(k\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise. For a nondeterministic rule, the sum counts the possible paths, and \\(k\\) is reachable when the count is positive. Could adding a path ever lower the weight of reaching a configuration? Not with any of these rules: none of their weights is negative, so every path adds something. Adding the weights of the paths that end at \\(0\\) gives the weight of ending there. Problems State the sum rule, and say when probabilities of paths may be added. Using the step that keeps the bit with probability \\(3/4\\), find \\(P(1\\mid0)\\) for two steps with the sum rule, and check that it and \\(P(0\\mid0)\\) sum to one. All weights in the three models are nonnegative. Explain why this means that adding paths can never lower a configuration’s weight, and why this matters for the next section."}, {"title": "States and steps", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-3", "text": "3.2.3 States and steps § So far each run started from a definite configuration. What if we know the configuration only with some probability? We then describe the register by a state : a weight \\(v_j\\) for each configuration \\(j\\). The definite configuration \\(i\\) is the state with weight \\(1\\) on \\(i\\) and \\(0\\) elsewhere; a probability distribution is a state whose weights are nonnegative and add to \\(1\\). How does one step change a state? The weight \\(v_i\\) travels along each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) and is multiplied by \\(w(i\\to j)\\), by the product rule. At \\(j\\), the arriving contributions add, by the sum rule: \\[v^\\prime_j=\\sum_i w(i\\to j)\\,v_i.\\] Under COIN, every probability distribution goes to the state with weights \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/2\\). When can a step be undone, so that a second rule brings every state back to where it started? For a deterministic rule, exactly when one arrow leaves and one arrow enters each configuration; reversing every arrow undoes it. The reversible gates are of this kind. CNOT, for example, acts on two bits by the arrows \\(00\\to00\\), \\(01\\to01\\), \\(10\\to11\\), and \\(11\\to10\\). RESET and COIN send different states to the same state. Nothing can undo them, because the result does not record where they started. COIN sends every distribution to the state with weights \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/2\\), so the state after the step does not determine the state before it. Problems Write the formula for one step of a rule applied to a state \\(v\\), and say which part comes from the product rule and which from the sum rule. Apply one step of the rule that keeps the bit with probability \\(3/4\\) and flips it with probability \\(1/4\\) to the distribution with weights \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/2\\). Then apply COIN to the same distribution. Which of NOT, RESET, and COIN can be undone? Answer using the arrows entering and leaving each configuration."}, {"title": "An operation that probability cannot describe", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-1", "text": "3.3.1 An operation that probability cannot describe § The bits in a computer are held by devices made of very many atoms. What happens when a bit is carried by a single atom? Choose two distinguishable conditions of the atom, such as two of its energy levels, and label them \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). A two-state system of this kind is a qubit , short for quantum bit. Looking at it, which is called a measurement , gives one of the two labels. Experiments with qubits reveal an operation, which we call \\(H\\), with the following behaviour. Applied once to \\(0\\) or to \\(1\\) and then measured, it gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). Applied twice to \\(0\\) and then measured, it gives \\(0\\) with certainty; applied twice to \\(1\\), it gives \\(1\\) with certainty. Applied, measured, applied again, and measured, it gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). Observation 1 looks familiar: one application of \\(H\\) behaves like COIN. Observation 2 does not. Can the rules we already have describe it? To put the question precisely, we assume as little as possible. Assumption 1. Each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) of \\(H\\) has a weight \\(H(i\\to j)\\), and repeated applications compose by the product and sum rules. The probability of a measurement outcome is \\(f(w)\\), where \\(w\\) is the weight of reaching that outcome, and \\(f(w)=0\\) exactly when \\(w=0\\). Proposition. No nonnegative weights satisfy observations 1 and 2. Proof. By observation 1, outcome \\(1\\) has nonzero probability after one step from \\(0\\) and after one step from \\(1\\), so \\(H(0\\to1)\\) and \\(H(1\\to1)\\) are nonzero, hence positive. The two paths from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) give the weight \\(H(0\\to0)H(0\\to1)+H(0\\to1)H(1\\to1)\\geq H(0\\to1)H(1\\to1)\\gt 0\\). Outcome \\(1\\) then has nonzero probability, contradicting observation 2. \\(\\square\\) If the product and sum rules hold at all, then, some weight of \\(H\\) must be negative, and the two paths from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) must cancel. Observation 3 adds a clue: when the qubit is measured between the steps, they do not cancel. Quantum mechanics is the physical theory of systems such as qubits. We now find its rules for a qubit, starting from Assumption 1 and the three observations. COIN matches a single application and an interrupted pair, but not two unobserved applications. The unobserved qubit keeps information that the probabilities do not. Problems State the three observations about \\(H\\), and Assumption 1. Using COIN, predict the result of two applications from \\(0\\). Which observation about \\(H\\) contradicts the prediction? The argument against a probabilistic description of \\(H\\) used runs starting from both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). Why would the run from \\(0\\) alone not rule out every probabilistic rule?"}, {"title": "From weights to probabilities", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-2", "text": "3.3.2 From weights to probabilities § Once weights can be negative, a weight is no longer a probability. How, then, do we obtain probabilities from weights? First some notation. The state of a qubit is a weight for each configuration, \\(v_0\\) and \\(v_1\\). We write it \\(v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\), where \\(\\ket0\\), read “ket zero,” stands for the definite configuration \\(0\\), and \\(\\ket1\\) for \\(1\\). When both weights are nonzero, the state is a superposition . What must the function \\(f\\) satisfy? A probability is never negative, so \\(f\\) must be nonnegative even for negative weights. And the probabilities of the two outcomes must add to \\(1\\) for every state an operation can produce. The simplest candidates are powers of the magnitude. Assumption 2. \\(f(w)=|w|^p\\) for some \\(p\\gt 0\\). Every operation must then leave \\(\\sum_j|v_j|^p\\) unchanged, whatever the state. Which operations do that? For most values of \\(p\\), very few. Theorem (quoted without proof). For \\(p\\neq2\\), the only operations that leave \\(\\sum_j|v_j|^p\\) unchanged for every state are those in which exactly one arrow leaves and exactly one arrow enters each configuration, with weight \\(+1\\) or \\(-1\\). Such an operation sends each configuration to a single configuration, so it can never produce the coin-like result of observation 1. Only \\(p=2\\) remains. Measurement rule. Measuring the state \\(v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\) gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(v_j^2\\), and \\(v_0^2+v_1^2=1\\). What does this demand of an operation? It must leave the total squared weight unchanged for every state. Start from the definite configuration \\(i\\): after one step the weights are \\(w(i\\to j)\\), so their squares must add to \\(1\\). Now start from the state with weight \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) on \\(i\\) and on another configuration \\(k\\). After one step, \\(j\\) has weight \\(\\bigl(w(i\\to j)+w(k\\to j)\\bigr)/\\sqrt2\\), and the total squared weight works out to \\(1+\\sum_jw(i\\to j)\\,w(k\\to j)\\). For this to be \\(1\\), the last sum must vanish. So every operation satisfies \\[\\sum_j w(i\\to j)^2=1,\\qquad \\sum_j w(i\\to j)\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\quad(i\\neq k).\\] These conditions have a consequence: every operation can be undone. Reverse each arrow and keep its weight. Applying the operation and then this reverse, the paths from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\) give the weight \\(\\sum_jw(i\\to j)\\,w(k\\to j)\\), which is \\(1\\) when \\(k=i\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise. Every configuration returns to where it started. Which of the rules we know qualify? A reversible deterministic rule satisfies both conditions, so every reversible gate is a valid operation on qubits. COIN does not: the squares of the weights leaving \\(0\\) add to \\(1/2\\). Nor does RESET: applied to \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), it gives the weight \\(1/\\sqrt2-1/\\sqrt2=0\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(0\\) at \\(1\\), so no outcome has any probability. With probability \\(|w|^p\\), every operation must preserve \\(\\sum_j|v_j|^p\\). Only \\(p=2\\) allows an operation that sends a configuration to an equal superposition. Problems Why can the probability of an outcome not equal its weight once weights may be negative? Using the measurement rule, find the outcome probabilities for the state \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0-(1/2)\\ket1\\) and check that they sum to one. Derive the condition \\(\\sum_jw(i\\to j)\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\) for \\(i\\neq k\\) from the requirement that every operation leaves the total squared weight unchanged."}, {"title": "The Hadamard step", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-3", "text": "3.3.3 The Hadamard step § We can now find the weights of \\(H\\) itself. Observation 1 says that one step from either configuration gives each outcome with probability \\(1/2\\). By the measurement rule, every weight has square \\(1/2\\), so each is \\(\\pm1/\\sqrt2\\). Which signs? Observation 2 decides. Two steps must return each configuration, so the paths from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) must cancel, and the paths from \\(0\\) to \\(0\\) must add to \\(1\\): \\[H(0\\to0)H(0\\to1)+H(0\\to1)H(1\\to1)=0,\\qquad H(0\\to0)^2+H(0\\to1)H(1\\to0)=1.\\] Since \\(H(0\\to1)\\neq0\\), the first equation gives \\(H(1\\to1)=-H(0\\to0)\\). The second gives \\(H(0\\to1)H(1\\to0)=1/2\\), so \\(H(0\\to1)=H(1\\to0)\\). The signs of \\(H(0\\to0)\\) and \\(H(0\\to1)\\) are left free; choosing both positive, \\[H(0\\to0)=H(0\\to1)=H(1\\to0)=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\qquad H(1\\to1)=-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\] so that \\[H\\ket0=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad H\\ket1=\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Are these weights a valid operation? The squares leaving each configuration add to \\(1/2+1/2=1\\), and \\(H(0\\to0)H(1\\to0)+H(0\\to1)H(1\\to1)=1/2-1/2=0\\). They are. Now the cancellation can be seen directly. Each two-step path from \\(0\\) has weight \\(\\pm1/2\\), and \\[\\text{weight of reaching }0=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12=1,\\qquad\\text{weight of reaching }1=\\tfrac12-\\tfrac12=0.\\] The paths to \\(1\\) cancel because the arrow \\(1\\to1\\) carries \\(-1/\\sqrt2\\). The arrows of COIN have the same size, but none is negative, so nothing can cancel. \\(H\\): state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). The paths to \\(0\\) add to weight \\(1\\); the paths to \\(1\\) cancel. Problems Read \\(H\\ket0\\) and \\(H\\ket1\\) from the weights of the arrows leaving \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). Draw the time-expanded diagram of two Hadamard steps from \\(1\\). Compute the path amplitudes to \\(0\\) and to \\(1\\), and the final probabilities. Using the paths from \\(1\\) to \\(0\\) and from \\(1\\) to \\(1\\) in two steps, check that the weights derived for \\(H\\) also return \\(1\\) with certainty."}, {"title": "Complex weights", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-1", "text": "3.4.1 Complex weights § Are negative weights enough? Ask a simple question: is there an operation that, applied twice, acts as NOT, half of a NOT? There must be one if operations come from physical processes that run continuously in time: stop the process halfway. Assumption 3. Every operation has a square root: an operation that, applied twice, equals it. NOT is a valid operation, since it is a reversible gate. Suppose \\(S\\) has real weights and two steps of \\(S\\) give NOT. The paths from \\(0\\) and from \\(1\\) give \\[S(0\\to0)^2+S(0\\to1)S(1\\to0)=0,\\qquad S(0\\to1)\\bigl(S(0\\to0)+S(1\\to1)\\bigr)=1,\\] \\[S(1\\to1)^2+S(1\\to0)S(0\\to1)=0,\\qquad S(1\\to0)\\bigl(S(0\\to0)+S(1\\to1)\\bigr)=1.\\] The two equations on the right show that \\(S(0\\to1)\\) and \\(S(1\\to0)\\) are nonzero with the same sign, so their product is positive. The first equation then asks for \\(S(0\\to0)^2\\lt 0\\). No real number has a negative square, so real weights are not enough. We need numbers whose squares can be negative. A complex number is \\(z=a+ib\\) with \\(a,b\\) real and \\(i^2=-1\\). Its conjugate is \\(z^*=a-ib\\), and its magnitude \\(|z|\\) satisfies \\(|z|^2=z^*z=a^2+b^2\\). In polar form \\(z=re^{i\\phi}\\), with \\(r=|z|\\), phase \\(\\phi\\), and \\(e^{i\\phi}=\\cos\\phi+i\\sin\\phi\\). Multiplication multiplies magnitudes and adds phases; \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\) have phases \\(0\\) and \\(\\pi\\). Complex weights are called amplitudes . What changes in our rules? A probability must be real and nonnegative, so in the measurement rule the square becomes the squared magnitude. Measurement rule. Measuring the state \\(v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\) gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|v_j|^2\\), and \\(|v_0|^2+|v_1|^2=1\\). Repeating the argument of the previous section with magnitudes, every operation satisfies \\[\\sum_j|w(i\\to j)|^2=1,\\qquad \\sum_j w(i\\to j)^*\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\quad(i\\neq k),\\] and is undone by reversing every arrow and conjugating its weight. An operation satisfying these conditions is unitary . And half of a NOT now exists. Take \\[S(0\\to0)=S(1\\to1)=\\frac{1+i}2,\\qquad S(0\\to1)=S(1\\to0)=\\frac{1-i}2.\\] It is unitary, and two steps of \\(S\\) give NOT: from \\(0\\), the weight of reaching \\(0\\) is \\(\\tfrac14\\bigl((1+i)^2+(1-i)^2\\bigr)=0\\), and the weight of reaching \\(1\\) is \\(\\tfrac14(1-i)\\cdot2(1+i)=1\\). The four kinds of rule now sit side by side. Along and across paths the arithmetic is the same; only the kind of weight changes. Rule Weight of an arrow Along a path Across paths Preserved by each step Deterministic \\(1\\); one arrow leaves each configuration Multiply At most one path One definite configuration Nondeterministic \\(1\\) for each allowed arrow Multiply Count None Probabilistic \\(P(j\\mid i)\\) Multiply Add \\(\\sum_jv_j\\) Quantum Complex amplitude Multiply Add; probability \\(=|\\text{amplitude}|^2\\) \\(\\sum_j|v_j|^2\\) The complex numbers \\(1\\), \\(i\\), \\(-1\\), and \\(-i\\) have magnitude one and phases \\(0\\), \\(\\pi/2\\), \\(\\pi\\), and \\(3\\pi/2\\). Multiplying complex numbers adds their phases. Problems Which two-step path sums show that an operation with real weights cannot equal NOT when applied twice? For the operation \\(S\\) with \\(S(0\\to0)=S(1\\to1)=(1+i)/2\\) and \\(S(0\\to1)=S(1\\to0)=(1-i)/2\\), compute the two-step weights from \\(1\\) to \\(0\\) and to \\(1\\). For \\(z=3+4i\\), find \\(z^*\\) and \\(|z|^2\\). Why does the measurement rule use \\(|v_j|^2\\) rather than \\(v_j^2\\) for complex amplitudes?"}, {"title": "Interference and phase", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-2", "text": "3.4.2 Interference and phase § When contributions to the same outcome reinforce one another, they interfere constructively ; when they cancel, partly or completely, they interfere destructively . In either case the probability of an outcome \\(k\\) is \\[p(k)=\\Bigl|\\sum_{\\text{paths to }k}\\text{path amplitude}\\Bigr|^2.\\] What if we squared each path first? For two applications of \\(H\\) from \\(0\\), we would get \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) for each outcome, which is exactly the prediction of COIN. The order matters: add, then square. Did the qubit pass through \\(0\\) or through \\(1\\) between the steps? The calculation does not say. Had it been in a definite configuration, we would see observation 3, not observation 2. The intermediate configuration is a label we sum over, not an event that took place. Paths whose amplitudes are added in this way are coherent alternatives . The probabilities still add to \\(1\\), so interference that raises one outcome lowers another. This is what a quantum algorithm exploits: it arranges its operations so that paths to correct answers reinforce and paths to wrong answers cancel. Which features of a state actually matter? Multiply every amplitude by the same \\(e^{i\\theta}\\). By the sum and product rules every later amplitude is multiplied by the same factor, and \\(|e^{i\\theta}z|=|z|\\), so no probability ever changes. Such a factor is a global phase , and states that differ by one are physically the same. A difference of phase between amplitudes, a relative phase , is another matter. Take \\[\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1\\bigr).\\] Measured directly, it gives \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) for every \\(\\phi\\). After \\(H\\), the two paths to \\(0\\) give the amplitude \\((1+e^{i\\phi})/2\\), so \\(p(0)=(1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). The relative phase is invisible at first and decisive one step later. The operation \\(Z\\), with \\(Z(0\\to0)=1\\) and \\(Z(1\\to1)=-1\\), shifts the relative phase by \\(\\pi\\). Applying \\(H\\), \\(Z\\), \\(H\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) gives \\(\\ket1\\), while applying \\(H\\) twice gives \\(\\ket0\\). Check your understanding What goes wrong if path amplitudes are squared before they are added? Compare your explanation Squaring first discards the phases, so nothing can cancel. The result is the probabilistic prediction, which assigns a nonzero probability to an outcome whose amplitudes cancel. The first two states differ only by a global sign and have the same output probabilities. Changing only one component changes the outcome after \\(H\\). Problems What is the difference between a global phase and a relative phase? Which of \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), \\(-(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), and \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) describe the same physical state? Apply \\(H\\) to each to check. After \\(H\\), the state \\((\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(0\\) with probability \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Which phases cannot be told apart this way, and what further operation would separate them?"}, {"title": "Path information and interference", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-3", "text": "3.4.3 Path information and interference § Measuring the qubit between the two steps destroys the cancellation. Does it take a measurement? Suppose instead that a second bit quietly copies the path. Let this record start at \\(0\\), and write two-bit configurations with the qubit first. Insert CNOT, with the qubit as control and the record as target, between the two applications of \\(H\\). \\(H\\) acts on the qubit only: \\[\\ket{00}\\xrightarrow{H}\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}\\bigr)\\xrightarrow{\\text{CNOT}}\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}\\bigr)\\xrightarrow{H}\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11}\\bigr).\\] Look at the amplitudes for qubit outcome \\(1\\): still \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). They now belong to different configurations, \\(10\\) and \\(11\\), because the record differs. The sum rule adds only paths that end in the same configuration, so nothing cancels, and the qubit gives \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\). Nobody looked at the record; its existence is enough. Can the damage be repaired? Apply CNOT once more, before the final \\(H\\). The record returns to \\(0\\) on both paths, the state is again \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\), and the final \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket{00}\\). This is uncomputation , here used to restore interference. In general it is a matter of degree. Identical records keep the interference, perfectly distinguishable records destroy it, and partly distinguishable records reduce it; Chapter 5 makes this precise. The practical lesson for quantum computation: any workspace that records which path was taken must be uncomputed before the paths are brought together. Copying the intermediate configuration into a record separates the paths, so their amplitudes no longer meet. Uncomputing the record restores the interference. Problems What does the CNOT in this subsection copy, and where does it put the copy? Check the final state \\(\\tfrac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11})\\) by applying \\(H\\) to the qubit in \\(\\ket{00}\\) and in \\(\\ket{11}\\) separately. The record is never read. Why does it still change the qubit’s statistics, and how does uncomputation restore them?"}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-1", "text": "Two contributions to one outcome are \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). What is the outcome probability? The total amplitude is zero, so the probability is zero. Adding the squared magnitudes separately would incorrectly give \\(1/2\\) for coherent alternatives."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-2", "text": "Starting in \\(\\ket0\\), apply \\(H\\), then \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\). Compare with applying \\(H\\) twice. \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) gives \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). The final \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket1\\). By contrast, applying \\(H\\) twice returns \\(\\ket0\\). The intermediate probabilities are \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/2\\) in both cases, but the relative phase changes the final result."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-3", "text": "A qubit starts in \\(\\ket0\\). Between two applications of \\(H\\), a CNOT copies the qubit into a record bit that starts at \\(0\\). What is the probability that the qubit shows \\(0\\) at the end? What changes if a second CNOT is applied before the final \\(H\\)? After the final \\(H\\) the state is \\(\\tfrac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11})\\), so the qubit shows \\(0\\) with probability \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\). With a second CNOT, the record returns to \\(0\\) before the final \\(H\\), the paths meet again, and the qubit shows \\(0\\) with certainty."}, {"title": "State vectors and ket notation", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-1", "text": "4.1.1 State vectors and ket notation § The state of a qubit is a pair of amplitudes, one for each configuration. A system with more configurations, such as two qubits with configurations \\(00,01,10,11\\), has one amplitude for each of them. Can we treat such a list as a single object? We can, as a column vector. List the configurations in a fixed order and write the amplitudes in that order. For a qubit, \\[\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\ket0=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\\ket1=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] The bracket in \\(\\ket\\psi\\) names a vector; it is not an operation. This is Dirac notation . The vectors \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) of the definite configurations form the computational basis . Every state is a combination of them, and the measurement rule reads its probabilities from the coordinates: \\(|\\alpha|^2\\) and \\(|\\beta|^2\\). The coordinates of a state vector therefore satisfy \\(|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1\\). Is “being in a superposition ” a property of the state alone? No. The state \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) is a superposition of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\), but it is itself a vector of another basis, as we will see. A superposition is always a superposition relative to some basis. With \\(n\\) qubits there are \\(2^n\\) configurations, so a state vector has \\(2^n\\) coordinates. The two coordinates are ordered by the selected basis. The coefficients belong to basis vectors, not to two simultaneous measurement outcomes. Problems What are the coordinate vectors of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) in the computational basis? Write \\(3\\ket0+4\\ket1\\) as a column vector. Which coordinate belongs to each basis vector? If two people write different coordinate vectors for the same state in different bases, are they making different physical claims? What must you know before comparing their lists of amplitudes?"}, {"title": "Operations as matrices", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-2", "text": "4.1.2 Operations as matrices § Applying an operation by path sums means writing out every arrow. Is there a more compact way? Collect the weights in a table whose entry in row \\(j\\) and column \\(i\\) is the weight of the arrow \\(i\\to j\\). This table is a matrix \\(U\\), with \\(U_{ji}=w(i\\to j)\\). One step changes the state by \\(v^\\prime_j=\\sum_iw(i\\to j)\\,v_i\\). In matrix language this is \\(v^\\prime_j=\\sum_iU_{ji}v_i\\), the matrix–vector product: \\(\\ket{\\psi^\\prime}=U\\ket\\psi\\). The sum and product rules for one step are exactly matrix multiplication. Column \\(i\\) of \\(U\\) holds the weights of the arrows leaving \\(i\\), so it is the state \\(U\\ket i\\). For the operations we have met, writing \\(X\\) for NOT and \\(\\operatorname{diag}(a,b)\\) for the matrix with diagonal entries \\(a,b\\) and zeros elsewhere, \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1),\\qquad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Two steps, first \\(U\\) and then \\(V\\), sum over the intermediate configuration \\(j\\): \\((VU)_{ki}=\\sum_jV_{kj}U_{ji}\\). This is the matrix product, with the rightmost matrix acting first. For example, \\(HH=I\\), the identity matrix. Why is a matrix determined by what it does to the basis states? Because \\(U(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)=\\alpha\\,U\\ket0+\\beta\\,U\\ket1\\): each configuration’s amplitude is carried along separately and the results add. This property, linearity , is the sum rule in another form. Which matrices are valid operations? The two conditions on the weights of an operation decide, and their compact form needs one more tool. The \\(X\\) operation is fixed by its two basis actions. Multiply those outputs by the input coefficients and add to obtain the output on a superposition. Problems What does linearity say about \\(U(a\\ket0+b\\ket1)\\)? Given \\(U\\ket0=\\ket1\\) and \\(U\\ket1=\\ket0\\), find \\(U\\) applied to \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Why does knowing an operator’s action on every basis vector determine its action on all superpositions? What could go wrong if a proposed rule were allowed to depend on how the same vector was decomposed?"}, {"title": "Inner products, bras, and adjoints", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-3", "text": "4.1.3 Inner products, bras, and adjoints § The conditions on an operation were \\(\\sum_j|w(i\\to j)|^2=1\\) and \\(\\sum_jw(i\\to j)^*\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\) for \\(i\\neq k\\). Since \\(w(i\\to j)=U_{ji}\\), these are statements about the columns of \\(U\\): each column has squared magnitudes adding to \\(1\\), and for two different columns, the conjugated entries of one times the entries of the other add to \\(0\\). Such sums deserve a name. The inner product of two vectors is \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j.\\] Why the conjugate? So that \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=\\sum_j|\\psi_j|^2\\) is real and nonnegative. Without it, the one-coordinate vector \\((i)\\) would have “squared length” \\(i^2=-1\\). The norm \\(\\|\\psi\\|=\\sqrt{\\braket\\psi\\psi}\\) is the length of \\(\\ket\\psi\\); a state vector has norm \\(1\\). Two vectors are orthogonal when their inner product is \\(0\\), and a basis of orthogonal unit vectors is orthonormal . The bra \\(\\bra\\phi\\) is the row of conjugated coordinates of \\(\\ket\\phi\\), so that the row \\(\\bra\\phi\\) times the column \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is the inner product \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). For example, \\(\\bra0=(1,0)\\), and \\(\\braket0\\psi=\\alpha\\) picks out the first coordinate. The conditions on an operation now say that the columns of \\(U\\) form an orthonormal basis. To write this as one equation, define the adjoint \\(U^\\dagger\\) of a matrix as its conjugate transpose: \\((U^\\dagger)_{ij}=U_{ji}^*\\). Then \\((U^\\dagger U)_{ik}=\\sum_jU_{ji}^*U_{jk}\\) is the inner product of columns \\(i\\) and \\(k\\), and the conditions say exactly \\[U^\\dagger U=I.\\] A matrix with this property is unitary , and \\(U^\\dagger\\) is its inverse: reverse each arrow and conjugate its weight. Is the adjoint always the inverse? No. For the matrix \\(\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\), which is not unitary, the adjoint is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\) itself, while the inverse is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1/2,1)\\). In general the adjoint is characterized by \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\): it moves a matrix from one side of an inner product to the other. For \\(v=(1,i)\\), the conjugate transpose is \\((1,-i)\\). Multiplying gives \\(1+1=2\\). Omitting conjugation would incorrectly give zero. Problems What extra operation distinguishes a complex inner product from an ordinary coordinate-wise product? Calculate the squared norm of \\((1,i)^T\\) by conjugating the first vector before multiplying. Find the adjoint of the diagonal matrix with entries \\(1\\) and \\(i\\). Multiply the two matrices and inspect the result."}, {"title": "State and evolution postulates", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-1", "text": "4.2.1 State and evolution postulates § For a qubit, the rules of quantum mechanics followed from a few observations and stated assumptions. Do they hold for every quantum system? Quantum mechanics asserts that they do. We adopt them as postulates : rules taken as the starting point of the theory and tested by experiment. Postulate 1 (states). A state of a quantum system is a unit vector in a complex vector space whose orthonormal basis corresponds to the configurations of the system. Vectors that differ only by a global phase represent the same state. Postulate 2 (evolution). An operation on a system that does not interact with anything else is a unitary matrix \\(U\\); it changes the state \\(\\ket\\psi\\) into \\(U\\ket\\psi\\). What does a general qubit state look like? Remove a global phase so that the first coordinate is real and nonnegative. The state then has the form \\[\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket1,\\] where the magnitudes fix \\(\\theta\\) and the relative phase is \\(\\phi\\). Is every preparation described by a single state vector? No. If we know only that a system was prepared in one of several states with given probabilities, or if it is part of a larger system it has interacted with, a different description is needed, the density matrix of Appendix D. The main text works with single state vectors. The vector \\((3,4)\\) has norm five. Dividing each coordinate by five gives probabilities \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\), which add to one. Problems What is the required norm of a pure-state vector? Normalize \\(3\\ket0+4\\ket1\\) and check that the resulting measurement probabilities sum to one. A vector and twice that vector point in the same direction. Why cannot both be used directly as normalized state vectors? Which part of the vector describes the state, and what role does normalization play in assigning probabilities?"}, {"title": "The measurement postulate", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-2", "text": "4.2.2 The measurement postulate § The measurement rule gives the probability of each outcome. What is the state after the measurement? Measure a qubit and obtain \\(0\\), then measure it again immediately. Experiments find \\(0\\) again, every time. Which state gives \\(0\\) with certainty? Only \\(\\ket0\\), up to a global phase. So after the outcome \\(0\\), the qubit is in the state \\(\\ket0\\). Postulate 3 (measurement). Measuring the state \\(\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\ket j\\) in the computational basis gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\alpha_j|^2\\), and leaves the system in the state \\(\\ket j\\). For \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), the outcome is \\(0\\) with probability \\(3/4\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/4\\); after the outcome \\(1\\), the state is \\(\\ket1\\). A single run gives a single outcome; the probabilities describe how often each outcome occurs. This also explains why measuring between two applications of \\(H\\) destroys their return to the start: after the measurement the qubit is \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket1\\), and \\(H\\) applied to either gives each outcome with probability \\(1/2\\). Is a measurement an operation in the sense of Postulate 2? No. When the outcome is \\(0\\), it sends both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) to \\(\\ket0\\). Different states merge, so a measurement cannot be undone and is not unitary. A measurement leaves the observed configuration, so an immediate second measurement repeats the result with certainty. Problems What does a computational-basis measurement return? For \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), calculate each outcome probability and state the conditional state after outcome 1. A measurement can have a multidimensional outcome subspace. Does observing its outcome determine a unique final vector? What information about the initial state can survive inside that subspace?"}, {"title": "Repeated measurements on independently prepared states", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-3", "text": "4.2.3 Repeated measurements on independently prepared states § How can a predicted probability be tested when a single run gives a single outcome? Prepare the same state many times, independently, and measure each copy. If an outcome has probability \\(p\\), its count in \\(N\\) runs has mean \\(Np\\) and variance \\(Np(1-p)\\). The observed fraction therefore fluctuates around \\(p\\) by about \\(\\sqrt{p(1-p)/N}\\), which shrinks as \\(1/\\sqrt N\\). A finite experiment estimates \\(p\\); it does not reproduce it exactly. Is measuring one system again the same as measuring a fresh copy? No. After a measurement the system is in the observed configuration, so an immediate second measurement repeats the result with certainty. Measuring \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\); measuring the same qubit again repeats that result, while a fresh copy again gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\). The counts of an experiment therefore mean something only once both the preparation and the measurement are specified. After observing zero in a computational-basis measurement, the same qubit remains in \\(\\ket0\\). A fresh \\(\\ket+\\) still gives an equal distribution. Problems What does “independently prepared copies” mean? Measure \\(\\ket+\\) in the computational basis and obtain 0. Compare the next measurement of that same qubit with a measurement of a fresh \\(\\ket+\\). Why is measuring one system repeatedly not generally equivalent to measuring many independently prepared copies? Which experiment would reveal the original preparation probabilities, and which would mostly test the state left by the first measurement?"}, {"title": "Changes of basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-1", "text": "4.3.1 Changes of basis § Is the computational basis special? Consider \\[\\ket+=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad\\ket-=\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Both are unit vectors, and \\(\\braket+-=\\tfrac12(1-1)=0\\), so they form another orthonormal basis. Every state can be written in this basis, and its coordinates are inner products: the coefficient of \\(\\ket+\\) in \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is \\(\\braket+\\psi\\). For example, \\(\\braket+0=\\braket-0=1/\\sqrt2\\), so \\[\\ket0=\\frac{\\ket++\\ket-}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Has anything physical changed? No. Writing \\(\\ket0\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis changes its coordinates, not the state. Applying a gate does change the state. The two can look alike on paper, so it matters to say which one is meant. The same holds for any orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\): \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_j\\braket{u_j}\\psi\\,\\ket{u_j}\\). These rows change coordinates without changing the physical state. The plus/minus basis is defined by \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Problems What are \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) in the computational basis? Add the expressions for \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to write \\(\\ket0\\) in that basis. Writing a state in a new basis and applying a gate can use similar matrix calculations. How would you distinguish a change in description from a physical change when predicting a fixed measurement?"}, {"title": "Measurement in an orthonormal basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-2", "text": "4.3.2 Measurement in an orthonormal basis § Apply \\(H\\) to a qubit and then measure it. Which property of the original state does the outcome reveal? \\(H\\) sends \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to \\(\\ket1\\). Write the original state as \\(a\\ket++b\\ket-\\); after \\(H\\) it is \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\). The measurement gives \\(0\\) with probability \\(|a|^2=|\\braket+\\psi|^2\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(|\\braket-\\psi|^2\\). “Apply \\(H\\), then measure” therefore measures the state in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Does this work for any orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\)? Let \\(U\\) be the unitary whose columns are the \\(\\ket{u_j}\\), so that \\(U\\ket j=\\ket{u_j}\\). Its inverse \\(U^\\dagger\\) sends \\(\\ket{u_j}\\) to \\(\\ket j\\). Applying \\(U^\\dagger\\) and then measuring gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\[|\\braket{j}{U^\\dagger\\psi}|^2=|\\braket{U j}\\psi|^2=|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2,\\] and applying \\(U\\) afterward turns the resulting state \\(\\ket j\\) into \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Measurement in a basis. Measuring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) in the orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\) gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2\\), and leaves the state \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Geometrically, \\(\\braket{u_j}\\psi\\,\\ket{u_j}\\) is the projection of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) onto the direction \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). With the projector \\(P_j=\\ket{u_j}\\bra{u_j}\\), the probability is \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\). More generally, a projective measurement is given by projectors \\(P_j\\) onto orthogonal subspaces with \\(\\sum_jP_j=I\\): outcome \\(j\\) has probability \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\) and leaves the state \\(P_j\\ket\\psi/\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|\\). When a subspace has more than one dimension, the outcome identifies the subspace, not a single vector. A real-coordinate illustration of the measurement rule. The projections are components used to calculate probabilities, not simultaneous fractional measurement results. Problems To measure a qubit in the basis \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket-\\}\\), which operation is applied before a computational-basis measurement, and why? Measure \\(\\ket0\\) in the basis \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket-\\}\\). Find the outcome probabilities and the state after each outcome. Let \\(U\\) be the unitary with columns \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Show that applying \\(U^\\dagger\\) and then measuring in the computational basis gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Overlaps under unitary evolution", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-3", "text": "4.3.3 Overlaps under unitary evolution § What does a unitary preserve? For any two states, \\[\\braket{U\\phi}{U\\psi}=\\braket{\\phi}{U^\\dagger U\\psi}=\\braket\\phi\\psi.\\] Lengths are preserved, so total probability stays \\(1\\), and so are the overlaps between different states. Since measurement probabilities are squared overlaps, no operation can make two states more distinguishable than they already are. Could we allow any matrix and rescale the result to unit length afterward? No. Rescaling by a factor that depends on the state is not linear: it would break the sum rule, and the parts of a superposition would no longer evolve independently. Measurement, by contrast, does not preserve overlaps. The states \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) have overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), yet after the outcome \\(0\\) both are \\(\\ket0\\). Measurement is the one place where the description leaves unitary evolution. An inner product between two inputs equals that between their unitary outputs. Orthogonal inputs stay orthogonal; nonorthogonal inputs do not become perfectly distinguishable. Problems What equation must \\(U\\) satisfy to be unitary? The matrix \\(X\\) swaps two coordinates. Apply it twice and use the result to identify its inverse. Unitary evolution is reversible, while a measurement outcome can leave several possible inputs indistinguishable. Why does this not justify describing measurement as just another unitary on the measured system alone?"}, {"title": "Eigenvectors and eigenspaces", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-1", "text": "4.4.1 Eigenvectors and eigenspaces § Some states are left almost unchanged by an operation. \\(Z\\) leaves \\(\\ket0\\) alone and multiplies \\(\\ket1\\) by \\(-1\\). A vector \\(\\ket v\\neq0\\) with \\(A\\ket v=\\lambda\\ket v\\) is an eigenvector of \\(A\\) with eigenvalue \\(\\lambda\\): \\(A\\) keeps its direction and multiplies it by \\(\\lambda\\). For \\(Z\\), the vectors \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) are eigenvectors with eigenvalues \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\). For \\(X\\), which exchanges \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\), we find \\(X\\ket+=\\ket+\\) and \\(X\\ket-=-\\ket-\\), with the same eigenvalues. What can the eigenvalues of a unitary be? A unitary preserves length, so \\(|\\lambda|=1\\). An eigenvector of a unitary only gains a phase. If several independent eigenvectors share an eigenvalue, every combination of them is again an eigenvector with that eigenvalue. Together they form an eigenspace . For \\(Z\\), the computational basis vectors are eigenvectors with eigenvalues \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\). A general superposition need not be an eigenvector. Problems What equation defines an eigenvector and its eigenvalue? \\(Z\\) maps \\(\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) to \\(-\\ket1\\). Identify the two eigenvalues. If two independent eigenvectors have the same eigenvalue, what happens to their superposition under the operator? Can the eigenvalue alone distinguish the two vectors or the superposition?"}, {"title": "Hermitian operators and spectral projections", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-2", "text": "4.4.2 Hermitian operators and spectral projections § A measurement often reports a number, such as an energy, rather than a label. How do we attach numbers to outcomes? Choose a real number \\(a_j\\) for each outcome of a projective measurement and form \\[A=\\sum_ja_jP_j.\\] The eigenspaces of \\(A\\) are the outcome subspaces, and its eigenvalues are the reported numbers. Such a matrix satisfies \\(A^\\dagger=A\\); it is Hermitian . Conversely, every Hermitian matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors, so grouping equal eigenvalues writes it in the form \\(A=\\sum_ja_jP_j\\), its spectral decomposition . A Hermitian matrix used in this way is an observable , and measuring it gives \\(a_j\\) with probability \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\). For example, \\(A=\\operatorname{diag}(2,5)\\) has eigenvectors \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) with eigenvalues \\(2\\) and \\(5\\). Measuring \\(A\\) is a computational-basis measurement that reports \\(2\\) or \\(5\\). Two observables with the same eigenspaces but different eigenvalues ask the same question and report different numbers. To measure an observable is to specify both. For \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(2,5)\\), the projectors select the two computational-basis components. The measurement reports the associated eigenvalue. Problems What makes an operator Hermitian? For the diagonal matrix with entries 2 and 5, identify its eigenvectors, eigenvalues, and computational-basis measurement projectors. Two Hermitian operators can share the same eigenspaces but assign them different eigenvalues. In what sense do their projective measurements ask the same question, and in what sense do they report different values?"}, {"title": "Nonorthogonal state discrimination", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-3", "text": "4.4.3 Nonorthogonal state discrimination § Someone hands us one qubit, prepared either in \\(\\ket0\\) or in \\(\\ket+\\). Can we always tell which? Suppose a measurement could. Some outcome would then occur with certainty for \\(\\ket0\\) and never for \\(\\ket+\\). By the measurement postulate, \\(\\ket0\\) would lie in that outcome’s subspace and \\(\\ket+\\) would be orthogonal to it, so \\(\\braket0+=0\\). But \\(\\braket0+=1/\\sqrt2\\). No measurement identifies the two states with certainty. Could a cleverer procedure help, such as an operation before measuring, or an interaction that writes a record into another system? Any such operation is unitary and preserves the overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), so the records cannot become orthogonal either. The limit is structural, not a weakness of detectors. Two pure states can be distinguished perfectly exactly when they are orthogonal. A measurement can trade wrong answers for answers that report “inconclusive,” but it cannot always be right. The real slice shown contains \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) at \\(45^\\circ\\). Their overlap is \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), so one copy cannot always reveal which was prepared. Angles here represent state-vector geometry, not physical space. Problems Can two nonorthogonal states always be distinguished perfectly from one copy? Calculate \\(\\braket{0}{+}\\). Does the result allow perfect discrimination of these two states? Nonorthogonal states are different, yet no measurement always identifies which one was supplied from a single copy. Why is mathematical distinctness weaker than perfect experimental distinguishability? Which requirement could you relax to obtain a different task?"}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-1", "text": "Does \\(U^\\dagger\\) always equal \\(U^{-1}\\)? No. The adjoint is the conjugate transpose. It equals the inverse when \\(U\\) is unitary. A nontrivial projection has an adjoint but no inverse on the whole space."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-2", "text": "Normalize \\(3\\ket0+4i\\ket1\\) and calculate the probabilities of a computational-basis measurement. The normalized state is \\((3/5)\\ket0+(4i/5)\\ket1\\). The probabilities are \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\). Conditional on outcome 1, the resulting ray is represented by \\(\\ket1\\); its overall phase has no observable effect."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-3", "text": "Why can no unitary map both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) to two orthogonal output states? Unitary transformations preserve inner products. Two orthogonal outputs have inner product zero, but these inputs have inner product \\(1/\\sqrt2\\). Such a map cannot be unitary. This is part of the obstruction to perfect discrimination of nonorthogonal states."}, {"title": "Tensor products and product bases", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-1", "text": "5.1.1 Tensor products and product bases § A register of two qubits has four configurations, \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\), so its state has four amplitudes. Suppose we prepare the first qubit in \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\) and, independently, the second in \\(c\\ket0+d\\ket1\\). What are the four joint amplitudes? Reaching the configuration \\(01\\) means that the first qubit is \\(0\\) and the second is \\(1\\). The two preparations are independent, so by the product rule the amplitude is the product of the two local amplitudes, \\(ad\\). Ordering the configurations with the first qubit as the leftmost bit, \\[\\begin{pmatrix}a\\\\b\\end{pmatrix}\\otimes\\begin{pmatrix}c\\\\d\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}ac\\\\ad\\\\bc\\\\bd\\end{pmatrix}.\\] This combination is the tensor product . For basis states it gives \\(\\ket a\\otimes\\ket b=\\ket{ab}\\); for example, \\(\\ket0\\otimes\\ket1=\\ket{01}\\). The four vectors \\(\\ket{ab}\\) form the product basis . Two qubits need four amplitudes and three qubits need eight: dimensions multiply rather than add. How does an operation on the first qubit alone act on the pair? It changes the first label and leaves the second unchanged: each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) of \\(U\\), with weight \\(w\\), becomes the arrows \\(ib\\to jb\\), with the same weight, for each value of \\(b\\). We write this operation \\(U\\otimes I\\). On a product state , \\((U\\otimes I)(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi)=(U\\ket\\psi)\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\). The joint basis has one label for every pair of local labels. This is the Cartesian arrangement of tensor-product basis vectors, not a mixture. Problems List the computational-basis labels for two qubits. Expand \\(\\ket0\\otimes(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) in the joint basis. Why does combining two two-dimensional systems require four joint basis labels rather than merely four alternatives to which system exists? What does a tensor-product label say about simultaneous properties of both systems?"}, {"title": "Product states", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-2", "text": "5.1.2 Product states § A joint state of the form \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\) is a product state . Its amplitudes are products of local amplitudes, so its probabilities are products of local probabilities: the two qubits behave independently. Does a superposition of several joint configurations signal something more? Not necessarily. The state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) has two nonzero amplitudes, yet \\[\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0:\\] the first qubit is in an equal superposition, and the second is certainly \\(0\\). The real question is whether one local vector for each qubit reproduces every joint amplitude. When it does, the state is a product state. Both terms share the same second-qubit state. Factoring it out shows that this pure state is a product, despite having two nonzero amplitudes. Problems What does it mean for a pure joint state to be a product state? Factor \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) into one state for each qubit. A product state may have several nonzero joint amplitudes. Why is counting those amplitudes an unreliable test for entanglement? What relationship among them would establish that the state factors?"}, {"title": "The composition postulate", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-3", "text": "5.1.3 The composition postulate § The product rule tells us how independently prepared systems combine. Must every joint state be a product? Quantum mechanics does not require it. It takes the whole tensor-product space as the state space of the pair. Postulate 4 (composition). The state space of a composite system is the tensor product of the state spaces of its parts. Its basis vectors are the products of basis vectors of the parts. A register of \\(n\\) qubits therefore has \\(2^n\\) basis vectors, and a general state needs \\(2^n\\) amplitudes. Does a measurement reveal \\(2^n\\) numbers? No: measuring all \\(n\\) qubits gives a single \\(n\\)-bit label. Storing a general \\(n\\)-qubit state on a classical computer takes up to \\(2^n\\) amplitudes, which quickly becomes impractical. Does that by itself make quantum computers powerful? No. An advantage needs a task, and a procedure that makes its answer observable efficiently. The number of basis coefficients doubles with each added qubit. One computational-basis measurement still returns only one n-bit label. Problems How many computational-basis states do three qubits have? For three qubits, compare the number of state-vector coordinates with the number of bits in one basis-measurement outcome. An \\(n\\)-qubit state has exponentially many coordinates, but a basis measurement returns only \\(n\\) bits. Why does the size of the mathematical description not by itself establish a computational advantage?"}, {"title": "Bell-state preparation", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-1", "text": "5.2.1 Bell-state preparation § Start from \\(\\ket{00}\\), apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit, then CNOT with the first qubit as control. What happens? \\(H\\) gives \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\), still a product state. CNOT acts on each configuration: it leaves \\(00\\) alone and sends \\(10\\) to \\(11\\): \\[\\ket{00}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] The result is a Bell state , written \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). The circuit drawing records this sequence, with time running from left to right. Does the vertical line of CNOT mean that the first qubit is measured and the result used to decide whether to flip the second? No. CNOT acts on both configurations of the superposition at once, without any measurement. That is why the result is a superposition of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), rather than one of them. A Hadamard followed by CNOT prepares a Bell state. Read the circuit from left to right. Predict first. Reset to \\(\\ket{00}\\). After \\(H\\) on the first qubit, which two outcomes are possible? Which outcomes remain after CNOT? Experiment: build a Bell state Follow the joint state from \\(\\ket{00}\\) through a Hadamard on the first qubit and then CNOT. The four bars show computational-basis probabilities. Apply H to the first qubit Reset State: \\(\\ket{00}\\) Both qubits start in 0. The final pure state is entangled: its joint amplitudes do not factor into two single-qubit states. Its probabilities alone would not distinguish it from a classical mixture. After trying it: compare your explanation After \\(H\\), \\(00\\) and \\(10\\) each have probability \\(1/2\\). CNOT changes \\(10\\) to \\(11\\), so the final outcomes are \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). The bars alone do not establish entanglement. Problems Which two gates prepare the Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\) in this subsection? Write the state after \\(H\\) on the first qubit and then after CNOT with that qubit as control. In Bell-state preparation, CNOT correlates the two qubits without measuring its control. How would the resulting preparation differ if the first qubit were measured after \\(H\\) and its outcome then used as an ordinary control?"}, {"title": "Nonfactorization of amplitudes", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-2", "text": "5.2.2 Nonfactorization of amplitudes § Can \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) be written as a product \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)\\otimes(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\)? Compare amplitudes: we would need \\(ac=bd=1/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(ad=bc=0\\). The first two equations make all four of \\(a,b,c,d\\) nonzero, and then \\(ad\\) cannot be \\(0\\). No product reproduces \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). A joint state that is not a product state is entangled . Neither qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) has a state vector of its own; only the pair does. Is entanglement just correlation? Measuring both qubits in the computational basis gives \\(00\\) or \\(11\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). A classical mixture, in which a coin decides whether to prepare \\(\\ket{00}\\) or \\(\\ket{11}\\), gives the same counts. The two differ in other measurements. A product \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\) would require \\(ac\\) and \\(bd\\) nonzero. Then all four factors are nonzero, contradicting \\(ad=bc=0\\). Problems What must fail for a pure two-qubit state to be entangled? Try factoring \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). Explain why nonzero \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) coefficients force unwanted cross terms in a product. Why is failure to factor amplitudes stronger evidence of pure-state entanglement than observing correlated outcomes in one basis? What comparison would expose the difference between a Bell state and a classical mixture?"}, {"title": "Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-3", "text": "5.2.3 Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities § How do we read probabilities from a joint state? With amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{ab}\\), the joint probability is \\(p(a,b)=|\\alpha_{ab}|^2\\). The probability that the first qubit gives \\(a\\), whatever the second gives, is the marginal \\(p(a)=\\sum_bp(a,b)\\), and the conditional probability of \\(b\\) given \\(a\\) is \\(p(b\\mid a)=p(a,b)/p(a)\\) when \\(p(a)\\gt 0\\). For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\); each qubit alone gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\); and once the first result is known, the second is certain. Now measure both qubits in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Rewriting \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) in that basis gives \\[\\ket{\\Phi^+}=\\frac{\\ket{++}+\\ket{--}}{\\sqrt2},\\] so the two results again agree with certainty. The classical mixture behaves differently. Each of \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\) is a product of states that give \\(+\\) and \\(-\\) with probability \\(1/2\\), so in the \\(\\pm\\) basis the mixture gives all four pairs of results with probability \\(1/4\\). Correlation in one basis is therefore not enough to establish entanglement; the measurement must be specified. For the Bell state, the row sums give the first-qubit probabilities. Conditional on the first bit being zero, only the \\(00\\) entry remains possible. Problems How do you obtain a marginal probability from joint probabilities? For \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\) and all other outcomes zero, calculate \\(p(\\text{first}=0)\\) and \\(p(\\text{second}=0 \\text{ given first}=0)\\). Knowing the first Bell measurement outcome lets you predict the second, although either local outcome is random. What changes when you condition on the first outcome: the local statistics available without the message, or the predictions available with it?"}, {"title": "Basis-state copying", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-1", "text": "5.3.1 Basis-state copying § Can a quantum state be copied? CNOT seems to copy. With a blank target, \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\to\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\to\\ket1\\ket1\\). What does it do to a superposition? By linearity, \\[(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\to\\alpha\\ket{00}+\\beta\\ket{11}.\\] Two copies would instead be \\[(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\otimes(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)=\\alpha^2\\ket{00}+\\alpha\\beta\\ket{01}+\\alpha\\beta\\ket{10}+\\beta^2\\ket{11}.\\] These differ whenever \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are both nonzero. CNOT copies the labels \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), not superpositions of them. The first two rows copy computational-basis labels into a blank target. By linearity the third row produces an entangled state rather than two copies of \\(\\ket+\\). Problems Which target value allows CNOT to copy a basis label? Apply CNOT to \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\), then use linearity to find its action on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\). Why does a device that copies both basis states fail to copy their arbitrary superposition? Expand the desired two-copy output and identify the terms that linear evolution does not produce."}, {"title": "The no-cloning theorem", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-2", "text": "5.3.2 The no-cloning theorem § Could some cleverer unitary copy every state? Suppose \\(U\\ket\\psi\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\) for every state \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Take two states \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(\\ket\\phi\\). A unitary preserves inner products, and the inner product of two product states is the product of the inner products of their parts, so \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi\\braket00=\\braket\\phi\\psi^2,\\qquad\\text{that is,}\\qquad\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\braket\\phi\\psi^2.\\] The overlap must therefore be \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). For two different states that are not orthogonal, such as \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) with overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), this fails. No unitary copies every state. This is the no-cloning theorem . What does the theorem not forbid? States known to belong to an orthogonal set, such as basis labels, can be copied, and a state that we know how to prepare can simply be prepared again. What is impossible is a single fixed operation that perfectly copies an arbitrary unknown state. Let \\(c=\\braket\\psi\\phi\\). A unitary cloning map would preserve input overlap \\(c\\), while two copies have overlap \\(c^2\\). The value \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) cannot satisfy that requirement. Problems Does the no-cloning theorem forbid copying known orthogonal basis labels? Compare CNOT’s output on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\) with \\(\\ket+\\ket+\\) by expanding both in the joint basis. What assumption in the no-cloning argument must change if the possible input states are known to be orthogonal? Why is a machine for copying that restricted set not a counterexample to the theorem?"}, {"title": "Local states and no-signalling", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-3", "text": "5.3.3 Local states and no-signalling § Alice and Bob share \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), Alice holding the first qubit and Bob the second. What state does Bob’s qubit have on its own? Not a state vector, since the pair is entangled. Whatever basis Bob measures in, each of his two outcomes has probability \\(1/2\\). These predictions are summarized by the density matrix \\(I/2\\), described in Appendix D. Can Alice change Bob’s statistics by acting on her own qubit? Suppose she applies a unitary to it, or measures it without telling Bob the result. Bob’s outcome probabilities do not change. If they could, Alice could send a message instantly by choosing her action. When Alice tells Bob her result, his description does change: given her result \\(0\\), his qubit is \\(\\ket0\\). But that information travels through an ordinary classical channel. Entanglement provides correlations, not a way to signal. For the Bell pair, either local computational-basis distribution is fair. Learning the remote outcome permits conditioning; without it, the local distribution remains unchanged. Problems What is the local outcome distribution for either half of a Bell state? If the other qubit is measured but its outcome is not sent to you, do your computational-basis probabilities change? Explain using the two possible outcomes. A distant measurement can change the state you assign after learning its outcome. Why can this not be used to send a chosen message without communicating the outcome? Distinguish conditional knowledge from locally observable statistics."}, {"title": "Entanglement resources", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-1", "text": "5.4.1 Entanglement resources § Alice holds a qubit in an unknown state \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) and wants Bob to have it. She cannot copy it, and measuring it would reveal only one bit. What can she do if they share a Bell pair? The three qubits start in \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\); Alice holds the first two and Bob the third. Alice applies CNOT from her input qubit to her half of the Bell pair, then \\(H\\) to her input qubit. Nothing quantum is sent to Bob. To see what has happened, we follow the complete three-qubit state. Alice and Bob first share a Bell pair. Alice’s joint measurement and two-bit message allow Bob to select the correction; the shared pair is consumed in transferring the input state. Problems What shared quantum resource does teleportation require? List the shared quantum resource and the classical communication used to teleport one qubit. Why must shared entanglement be included among the resources used by teleportation? If preparing and distributing the entangled pair were costly, how would that change an assessment of the protocol?"}, {"title": "Measurement and conditional corrections", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-2", "text": "5.4.2 Measurement and conditional corrections § Start from \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). CNOT flips Alice’s Bell half where the input is \\(1\\), and \\(H\\) then acts on the input qubit: \\[\\frac{\\alpha\\ket0(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})+\\beta\\ket1(\\ket{10}+\\ket{01})}{\\sqrt2}\\;\\to\\;\\frac{\\alpha(\\ket0+\\ket1)(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})+\\beta(\\ket0-\\ket1)(\\ket{10}+\\ket{01})}{2}.\\] Group the terms by the configuration of Alice’s two qubits: \\[\\tfrac12\\Bigl[\\ket{00}(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)+\\ket{01}(\\alpha\\ket1+\\beta\\ket0)+\\ket{10}(\\alpha\\ket0-\\beta\\ket1)+\\ket{11}(\\alpha\\ket1-\\beta\\ket0)\\Bigr]=\\frac12\\sum_{m,n\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\ket{mn}\\otimes X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi.\\] Alice now measures her two qubits. Each outcome \\((m,n)\\) has probability \\(1/4\\) and leaves Bob’s qubit in \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\). Bob does not know which. If Alice sends him the two bits \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), he applies \\(X^n\\) and then \\(Z^m\\). Since \\(Z^mX^nX^nZ^m=I\\), his qubit ends in \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Does teleportation depend on a lucky outcome? No: the calculation covers all four outcomes, and each one is corrected. Alice’s two measured bits control Bob’s \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) corrections. Dashed lines carry classical information; the Bell pair is prepared at the left. Problems Why does the receiver apply a correction in teleportation? If the receiver’s conditional state is \\(X\\ket\\psi\\), which gate recovers \\(\\ket\\psi\\)? Check by applying it. Before the classical correction, the receiver’s state depends on the sender’s measurement outcome. Why is knowing which correction to apply essential even though each possible correction is reversible?"}, {"title": "State transfer and classical communication", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-3", "text": "5.4.3 State transfer and classical communication § Could Bob use his qubit before the two bits arrive? Without them, his qubit is in each of the four states \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\) with probability \\(1/4\\). Averaged over the four, his predictions are those of a fair coin, \\(I/2\\), whatever \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is. He learns nothing about \\(\\ket\\psi\\), and nothing has travelled faster than the classical bits. Has the state been copied? No. Alice’s measurement leaves her qubits in \\(\\ket m\\ket n\\), with no trace of \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\), and the Bell pair has been used up. The unknown state now exists only at Bob, consistent with the no-cloning theorem. Teleportation places three descriptions side by side: the joint state of all three qubits, Bob’s state given Alice’s result, and Bob’s state when the result is unknown. Keeping them apart is the key to reasoning about entanglement. The two-bit message selects a correction using a previously shared Bell pair. It is not a classical list of the unknown amplitudes. Problems How many classical bits are sent in single-qubit teleportation? Explain why receiving the classical bits alone, without the shared Bell pair, is not the teleportation protocol. Teleportation communicates only two classical bits while transferring an arbitrary qubit state. Why does that not mean the two bits contain a classical description of all its amplitudes? What other resources make the transfer possible?"}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01})/\\sqrt2\\) entangled? No. It is \\(\\ket0\\otimes(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), a product state. A sum of basis states is not automatically entangled."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-2", "text": "Apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit of \\(\\ket{00}\\), then CNOT with that qubit as control. Give joint and first-qubit probabilities in the computational basis. The intermediate state is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) and the final state is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). The joint probabilities are \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\) and \\(p(01)=p(10)=0\\). Summing over the second bit gives \\(p(\\text{first}=0)=p(\\text{first}=1)=1/2\\)."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-3", "text": "Compare measuring both qubits in the \\(X\\) basis for the Bell state \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) and for an equal classical mixture of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\). For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), the outcomes \\({+}{+}\\) and \\({-}{-}\\) each have probability \\(1/2\\). The mixture yields \\({+}{+}\\), \\({+}{-}\\), \\({-}{+}\\), and \\({-}{-}\\) with probability \\(1/4\\) each. Their computational-basis counts agree, but these \\(X\\)-basis counts distinguish coherence from the mixture."}, {"title": "Constant and balanced Boolean functions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-1", "text": "6.1.1 Constant and balanced Boolean functions § Here is a small question about a function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\): is \\(f(0)\\) equal to \\(f(1)\\)? If they are equal, \\(f\\) is constant ; if they differ, \\(f\\) is balanced . For a one-bit input, every function is one or the other. What do we actually need to learn? One bit: equal or different. We do not need the values \\(f(0)\\) and \\(f(1)\\) themselves. Can an algorithm learn that one bit without learning both values? The gap between the answer we want and the full data is what the quantum algorithm will exploit. For inputs of more bits, a function need not be constant or balanced. Restricting attention to those two cases is then a promise about the input. Deutsch’s task asks whether the two outputs agree. It does not require identifying which truth table in a class was supplied. Problems When is a one-bit Boolean function constant? Classify the tables \\((f(0),f(1))=(0,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\), \\((1,0)\\), and \\((1,1)\\) as constant or balanced. For a one-bit function, the task asks whether two values agree, not what each value is. How can changing the requested answer change the information a successful algorithm must obtain?"}, {"title": "Classical query access", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-2", "text": "6.1.2 Classical query access § Suppose the only access to \\(f\\) is a query : we choose \\(x\\) and receive \\(f(x)\\). How many queries does a classical algorithm need to answer with certainty? After one query, say with the reply \\(f(0)=0\\), both possibilities remain: \\(f(1)=0\\) makes \\(f\\) constant, and \\(f(1)=1\\) makes it balanced. One query cannot settle the question; two clearly suffice. This count belongs to the stated access model. If \\(f\\) were given in another form, for instance as a formula we could inspect, the comparison could change. If a classical query returns \\(f(0)=0\\), both a constant and a balanced truth table remain possible. A second value is needed for an exact classical answer. Problems How many values does one ordinary classical query reveal? You queried \\(f(0)\\) and obtained 0. Write one constant and one balanced function consistent with that observation. After one classical query, which two possible truth tables remain indistinguishable while belonging to different classes? Why does this pair obstruct an exact one-query decision procedure?"}, {"title": "Reversible quantum oracles", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-3", "text": "6.1.3 Reversible quantum oracles § How can a quantum computation query \\(f\\)? A query must be a valid operation, hence unitary , while \\(f\\) itself may merge its inputs. The reversible embedding of a function solves this: keep the input and add \\(f(x)\\) to a target bit. \\[U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,\\,y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] On the four configurations, \\(U_f\\) is a rearrangement: it exchanges \\((x,0)\\) and \\((x,1)\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\) and leaves them alone otherwise. Its matrix is a permutation matrix, so it is unitary, and applying it twice restores every configuration. What counts as one query? One use of \\(U_f\\), whatever state it is applied to, including a superposition . This is the black-box , or oracle , model: it counts the uses of \\(U_f\\) and ignores how \\(U_f\\) is built. The construction costs return when circuits are analysed in detail. For \\(f(x)=1\\), the two target values are swapped while \\(x\\) is kept. The full map is reversible even when the function \\(f\\) itself is not one-to-one. Problems What is the reversible oracle’s action on \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{t}\\)? For \\(f(0)=1\\), find the oracle outputs on \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\). Why must the oracle specify what happens to its target register, not merely declare that it “returns \\(f(x)\\)”? How does retaining \\(x\\) permit a reversible operation even when \\(f\\) is many-to-one?"}, {"title": "Oracle action on superpositions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-1", "text": "6.2.1 Oracle action on superpositions § What happens if we query \\(f\\) on both inputs at once? Prepare the input in \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and the target in \\(\\ket0\\). By linearity, \\(U_f\\) acts on each configuration separately: \\[U_f\\left(\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\right)=\\frac{\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Have we computed both values? Both appear in the state, but nothing has read them. Whether this helps depends on what we do next. With \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\), a blank target becomes correlated with the input. Linearity determines the output on the entire superposition. Problems Which rule lets us find an oracle’s action on a superposition from its action on basis states? For \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\), apply the oracle to \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\) by expanding the input into two terms. An oracle acts on every basis component of a superposition. Why is that not equivalent to receiving a readable list of all function values? Which subsequent step determines whether this action is useful?"}, {"title": "Register measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-2", "text": "6.2.2 Register measurement § Suppose we measure both registers now. The result is one random pair \\((x,f(x))\\): we learn one value, not both. Repeating the experiment gives more samples, but that is no better than classical queries. To gain anything, the remaining steps must turn the property we want, equal or different, into states that a measurement can tell apart with certainty. For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), a computational-basis measurement samples one of these four labels. It does not output the full list of amplitudes. Problems Does one basis measurement return every amplitude of a register? For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), list the possible measured pairs and their probabilities. If measuring the input register returns one sampled input, what becomes of the other function values encoded in the joint state? Why does the existence of those components not guarantee access to all of them in one run?"}, {"title": "Workspace entanglement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-3", "text": "6.2.3 Workspace entanglement § Could we apply \\(H\\) to the input and measure, as in the two-step experiment with \\(H\\)? Look at the target. If \\(f(0)\\neq f(1)\\), the target holds different values in the two branches: it is a record of which input occurred. Branches that end in different configurations cannot cancel. Applying \\(H\\) to the input alone then gives a random outcome, just as when a separate bit records the path between two applications of \\(H\\). Is there a target state that the oracle leaves unchanged, so that no record forms, yet that still responds to \\(f\\)? Different workspace records can prevent the desired input interference. The shared-workspace example factors; the branch-record example does not. The common normalization factor \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) is omitted in the labels. Problems What is a workspace record? Compare \\((\\ket0\\ket0+\\ket1\\ket0)/\\sqrt2\\) with \\((\\ket0\\ket0+\\ket1\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). In which state does the second register distinguish the first-register alternatives? Two input branches leave different workspace records. Why can that obstruct the interference you wanted between them? What would have to be true of the final workspace for it to stop distinguishing the branches?"}, {"title": "Bit-flip eigenstates", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-1", "text": "6.3.1 Bit-flip eigenstates § The oracle either flips the target or leaves it alone. Which target states does a flip leave unchanged, apart from a factor? The eigenvectors of \\(X\\): \\[X\\ket+=\\ket+,\\qquad X\\ket-=-\\ket-,\\] since \\(X(\\ket0-\\ket1)=\\ket1-\\ket0\\). A flip therefore multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\), and no flip leaves it with the factor \\(+1\\): the target state stays the same while the factor records whether a flip occurred. The state \\(\\ket+\\) is unchanged in both cases and cannot tell them apart. The target we want is \\(\\ket-\\). A bit flip leaves \\(\\ket+\\) unchanged but multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\). Using the latter as the target makes a controlled flip supply a phase to the input branch. Problems What are the \\(X\\) eigenvalues of \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\)? Apply \\(X\\) to \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and factor out the sign. Both \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) are eigenstates of the bit flip. Why does only one of them provide the sign change needed here? What property of the eigenvalue, rather than simply being an eigenstate, matters?"}, {"title": "Phase-oracle construction", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-2", "text": "6.3.2 Phase-oracle construction § Apply \\(U_f\\) to \\(\\ket x\\ket-\\). If \\(f(x)=0\\), nothing happens; if \\(f(x)=1\\), the target flips, which multiplies the state by \\(-1\\): \\[U_f\\ket x\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-.\\] With the input in \\(\\ket+\\), \\[U_f\\ket+\\ket-=\\frac{(-1)^{f(0)}\\ket0+(-1)^{f(1)}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket-.\\] The target ends in \\(\\ket-\\) whatever \\(x\\) is, so it holds no record of the input. The function value has moved into the sign of the input amplitude . This is phase kickback . What do the signs mean? If \\(f\\) is constant, the two signs are equal and the input is \\(\\pm\\ket+\\); the common sign is a global phase . If \\(f\\) is balanced, the signs differ and the input is \\(\\pm\\ket-\\). The answer has become a relative phase . The target returns to the same minus state, while the input receives a function-dependent phase. Problems What phase factor is attached to input \\(x\\) by a phase oracle? For \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\), apply the phase factors to \\(\\ket+\\) and identify the new input state. Complementing every value of a Boolean function changes every phase mark by a common minus sign. Which distinctions between functions are therefore unavailable from this phase action alone?"}, {"title": "Coherent uncomputation", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-3", "text": "6.3.3 Coherent uncomputation § What if \\(f\\) is not given as an oracle but computed by a circuit \\(A\\) that uses workspace, \\(A\\ket{x,0,0}=\\ket{x,f(x),g(x)}\\)? Apply \\(Z\\) to the answer bit, which multiplies the state by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\), then run \\(A\\) backward: \\[A^\\dagger Z_{\\mathrm{answer}}A\\ket{x,0,0}=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x,0,0}.\\] The workspace returns to \\(0\\), and the sign remains. This is compute, phase, uncompute . It is not free: it uses \\(A\\) and its inverse, and an honest count of the cost must include both. For a basis input \\(x\\), compute \\(f(x)\\) into clean workspace, apply \\(Z\\) to that bit, and reverse the computation. The function-dependent sign remains while the workspace returns to zero. Problems What operation clears workspace by undoing its computation? A reversible computation maps \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{0}\\) to \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{g(x)}\\). Write the action of its inverse on that output. Why is erasing workspace by measurement generally different from uncomputing it coherently? What record or disturbance could remain after the first method that the second is designed to avoid?"}, {"title": "Interference and readout", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-1", "text": "6.4.1 Interference and readout § After the query, the input is \\((s_0\\ket0+s_1\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), with \\(s_x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\). How do we read the relative sign? Apply \\(H\\), which sends \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to \\(\\ket1\\): \\[H\\,\\frac{s_0\\ket0+s_1\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{s_0+s_1}2\\ket0+\\frac{s_0-s_1}2\\ket1.\\] Equal signs give \\(0\\) with certainty; opposite signs give \\(1\\) with certainty. In terms of paths, the two contributions to the wrong answer cancel. The outcome depends only on whether \\(s_0\\) and \\(s_1\\) agree, never on \\(f(0)\\) or \\(f(1)\\) separately. We learn the one bit we wanted, and nothing more. Ignoring a common global sign, constant functions leave the input in \\(\\ket+\\) and balanced functions produce \\(\\ket-\\). The final \\(H\\) converts this distinction into a definite basis outcome. Problems What role does the last Hadamard play in Deutsch’s algorithm? Calculate \\(H\\ket+\\) and \\(H\\ket-\\). Match the two results to constant and balanced functions. The oracle encodes the distinction in signs, but the final measurement reads a bit. What exactly does the last Hadamard do to bridge those descriptions? Why would measuring before it fail?"}, {"title": "Quantum and classical query counts", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-2", "text": "6.4.2 Quantum and classical query counts § Put the steps together. Prepare \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\) and apply \\(H\\) to each qubit , giving \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\). Query \\(U_f\\) once. Apply \\(H\\) to the input, and measure it. This is Deutsch’s algorithm . It decides whether \\(f\\) is constant or balanced with one query and with certainty, where any classical method needs two. Is the comparison fair? Both use the same oracle and demand the same certainty. The other gates are real operations, but they are not queries. A query separation is a first example of quantum advantage , not a statement about total running time on real hardware. Deutsch’s algorithm: prepare, query, recombine, and measure. Only the joint U_f box counts as an oracle query. Predict first. Compare a constant function with a balanced function. Before running the circuit, predict whether the final input bit is 0 or 1. Would complementing every function value change that bit? Experiment: an oracle changes relative phase Prepare the input in \\(\\ket{+}\\) and the target in \\(\\ket{-}\\). The oracle leaves the target unchanged and multiplies each input amplitude by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\). A final Hadamard makes the relative sign observable. Function f(0) = 0, f(1) = 0 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 0, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 0 Apply oracle Reset |0⟩ +1/√2 |1⟩ +1/√2 Both input amplitudes have the same sign. Choose a function, then apply its oracle. A common sign changes no probabilities. Opposite signs change the result after the final Hadamard. After trying it: compare your explanation A constant function gives 0; a balanced function gives 1. Complementing the function multiplies the state after the phase oracle by a common minus sign, so it does not change the final measurement. Problems How many oracle calls does the ideal Deutsch algorithm use? Compare one quantum call with the two calls required by an exact classical method. Name one cost this count does not include. Deutsch’s algorithm uses one quantum query where exact classical computation needs two. Which access and success assumptions make that comparison meaningful? What extra information would be needed to compare total running times?"}, {"title": "Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-3", "text": "6.4.3 Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement § What made the algorithm work? Four stages: prepare coherent alternatives; encode the property we want in their relative phases; recombine them; and measure in a basis that exposes the difference. Each stage matters. Without the \\(\\ket-\\) target, a record would block the interference ; without the final \\(H\\), the signs would stay invisible. A short circuit can still fail if the final states for different answers cannot be told apart, so correctness requires following the state through all four stages, workspace included. Searching for a marked input starts from the same kind of phase mark, but needs repeated recombination to turn the mark into a likely answer. The target \\(\\ket-\\) lets the oracle encode a phase without keeping an input-dependent target record. The final \\(H\\) converts that phase into the answer. Problems Which target state is prepared for phase kickback? Replace \\(\\ket-\\) with \\(\\ket+\\) and use \\(X\\ket+=\\ket+\\). Does the target flip now give the input a function-dependent sign? If the oracle is correct but the target is prepared in \\(\\ket+\\) instead of \\(\\ket-\\), where does the algorithm lose its ability to distinguish the functions? What does this reveal about treating preparation as part of correctness?"}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-1", "text": "Why use \\(\\ket-\\) rather than \\(\\ket0\\) as the target for phase kickback? A flip changes \\(\\ket-\\) only by a sign. The same target state remains, while the input amplitudes acquire function-dependent signs. A \\(\\ket0\\) target instead records the function value and can become entangled with the input."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-2", "text": "Take \\(f(0)=1\\) and \\(f(1)=0\\). Starting with \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), apply the oracle and then \\(H\\) to the input. What is measured on the input? The oracle gives \\(-\\ket-\\ket-\\). The final \\(H\\) on the input gives \\(-\\ket1\\ket-\\). The overall minus sign changes no probabilities, so outcome 1 occurs with certainty: the function is balanced."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-3", "text": "Replace \\(f\\) by its complement \\(1-f\\). Can Deutsch’s final measurement tell them apart? No. All input amplitudes gain the same extra minus sign, a global phase. The constant/balanced classification is unchanged. Deutsch’s measurement extracts that classification, not the individual truth table."}, {"title": "Search predicates and classical queries", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-1", "text": "7.1.1 Search predicates and classical queries § Among \\(N\\) labels, some are marked, and a predicate \\(f\\) tells us whether a label \\(x\\) is marked: \\(f(x)=1\\) if it is, and \\(0\\) otherwise. We want to find a marked label. How many queries does a classical search need? With one marked label in an unknown position, each query tests one candidate. Checking labels one by one may take all \\(N\\) queries in the worst case, and about \\(N/2\\) on average. To succeed with a fixed probability, a classical search needs a number of queries proportional to \\(N\\). We start with \\(N=4\\) and mark the label \\(11\\). As before, we count queries to \\(f\\) and keep the other operations in view. The task is to output a marked label, not to estimate how many there are. In this four-item example only \\(11\\) is marked. Testing a supplied candidate is different from locating the successful one. Problems What does a search predicate tell you about a candidate? Among eight equally likely candidates, two are marked. What is the success probability of one uniformly random guess? Why does a fast test of a candidate not automatically give a fast way to find a successful candidate? What additional structure would allow classical search to do better than inspecting unrelated possibilities?"}, {"title": "State preparation and phase marking", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-2", "text": "7.1.2 State preparation and phase marking § A quantum search can begin with all candidates at once. Two Hadamards prepare the uniform superposition \\[\\ket s=\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11}}2.\\] How should a query act? Phase kickback turns a query into a sign: \\[O_f\\ket x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x.\\] With \\(11\\) marked, the amplitudes become \\((1,1,1,-1)/2\\). Has this helped? Not yet. Every probability is still \\(1/4\\), and measuring now finds \\(11\\) with probability \\(1/4\\), as a guess would. The query has created a relative sign, and a further operation must turn that sign into probability. The phase oracle \\(O_f\\) may be supplied directly, or built by phase kickback or by compute, phase, uncompute. Its cost in queries must be stated accordingly. The marked item is \\(11\\). Every immediate measurement probability stays \\(1/4\\), so another coherent operation is needed to turn the sign into an advantage. Problems Does changing an amplitude’s sign change its squared magnitude? Start with four amplitudes of \\(1/2\\) and negate the marked one. Calculate its probability before and after marking. A phase mark leaves all immediate basis-measurement probabilities unchanged. Why can it still be a useful search operation? What must the next operation make those signs interact with?"}, {"title": "Reflection and final amplitudes", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-3", "text": "7.1.3 Reflection and final amplitudes § Which operation turns a negative sign into a larger amplitude ? Look at the amplitudes \\((1/2,1/2,1/2,-1/2)\\). Their mean is \\(\\mu=1/4\\). The marked amplitude lies far below the mean, and the others slightly above it. Reflect every amplitude about the mean, \\(a_x\\mapsto2\\mu-a_x\\): \\[\\tfrac12\\mapsto2\\cdot\\tfrac14-\\tfrac12=0,\\qquad-\\tfrac12\\mapsto2\\cdot\\tfrac14+\\tfrac12=1.\\] One marking and one reflection find \\(11\\) with certainty. Is reflection about the mean a valid quantum operation? It is the matrix \\[D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I.\\] Indeed, \\(\\braket s a=\\sum_xa_x/\\sqrt N\\), and every entry of \\(\\ket s\\) is \\(1/\\sqrt N\\), so the entries of \\(2\\ket s\\braket sa\\) are \\(2\\mu\\), and \\(D\\ket a\\) has entries \\(2\\mu-a_x\\). Since \\(D^2=4\\ket s\\bra s-4\\ket s\\bra s+I=I\\) and \\(D^\\dagger=D\\), it is unitary . It does not estimate the mean from samples; it acts on the amplitudes directly. A complete two-qubit search for 11. The first controlled-Z marks 11. The remaining coherent gates implement diffusion up to global phase. Both measured bits are one. Problems What is the reflection-about-the-mean rule for an amplitude \\(a\\)? For amplitudes \\((-1/2,1/2,1/2,1/2)\\), find the mean and apply \\(2\\cdot\\text{mean}-a\\) to each entry. For four items, one reflection after marking can make the marked amplitude one. Why does the same single iteration not generally give certainty for a larger search space? Track how the mean changes."}, {"title": "Good and bad subspaces", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-1", "text": "7.2.1 Good and bad subspaces § With \\(N\\) labels and \\(M\\) of them marked, \\(0\\lt M\\lt N\\), there are too many amplitudes to follow one by one. Is there a simpler description? In the uniform start, and after every marking and reflection, all marked labels have equal amplitudes, and so do all unmarked ones. So group them. Let \\(\\ket G\\) be the normalized equal superposition of the marked labels, and \\(\\ket B\\) that of the unmarked labels. They are orthogonal, because no label belongs to both. The uniform state lies in their plane: \\[\\ket s=\\sqrt{M/N}\\,\\ket G+\\sqrt{1-M/N}\\,\\ket B.\\] We never need to construct \\(\\ket G\\) or \\(\\ket B\\) explicitly. They describe the state, and they let us prove what the operations do. For one marked item among four, \\(\\ket{G}=\\ket{11}\\) and \\(\\ket{B}=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt3\\). These orthogonal directions contain the initial state and the entire ideal search trajectory. Problems What distinguishes the good subspace from the bad subspace? In a four-item search with only \\(00\\) marked, identify the good basis state and list the bad basis states. Why can a search over many basis states be described as motion in a two-dimensional plane? Which symmetries of the preparation and operations keep the state inside that plane?"}, {"title": "Initial success amplitude", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-2", "text": "7.2.2 Initial success amplitude § Where does \\(\\ket s\\) lie in this plane? Write \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{M/N}\\), with \\(0\\lt\\theta\\lt\\pi/2\\). Then \\[\\ket s=\\sin\\theta\\,\\ket G+\\cos\\theta\\,\\ket B:\\] the state makes the angle \\(\\theta\\) with the bad direction, and its success probability is \\(\\sin^2\\theta=M/N\\). For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), \\(\\sin\\theta=1/2\\), so \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), or \\(30^\\circ\\). Why is one angle enough, however many labels the register has? Both the oracle and the reflection map the plane to itself, so the state never leaves it. With one marked item among four, \\(\\sin\\theta=1/2\\), so \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\). The good amplitude is the vertical component; the success probability is its square. Problems If the initial success probability is \\(p\\), what is the nonnegative good amplitude? For one marked item among four equally weighted items, find \\(p\\) and \\(\\sqrt p\\). Use \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt p\\) to find \\(\\theta\\). Two search problems have the same number of items but different marked fractions. Why do they begin at different angles to the bad direction? How does that alter the amount of rotation needed?"}, {"title": "Reflections and rotations", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-3", "text": "7.2.3 Reflections and rotations § What does the oracle do in this plane? It negates \\(\\ket G\\) and keeps \\(\\ket B\\): a reflection across the bad axis. The operation \\(D\\) is a reflection across \\(\\ket s\\). Two reflections in a plane make a rotation, by twice the angle between their axes. Here the axes are the bad direction and \\(\\ket s\\), at the angle \\(\\theta\\), so each Grover iteration \\(DO_f\\) rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\) toward the good direction. For \\(N=4\\), the state starts at \\(30^\\circ\\). The oracle reflects it to \\(-30^\\circ\\), and the reflection across \\(\\ket s\\) sends it to \\(90^\\circ\\), the good direction: the certain success found above. The picture also predicts what happens if we continue. The rotation does not stop at the good direction; it carries the state past it. For \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\), the initial state is at \\(30^\\circ\\). The oracle reflects it to \\(-30^\\circ\\) about the bad axis; reflection about the initial direction then sends it to \\(90^\\circ\\). These are real coordinates in the good/bad plane. Problems What geometric operation results from composing the two search reflections? For \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), start at angle \\(\\theta\\) and add \\(2\\theta\\) for one Grover iteration. What angle do you reach? Two reflections produce a rotation, but reversing their order reverses its direction. Why must an algorithm specify the order of the marking and preparation reflections rather than merely list them?"}, {"title": "Amplitude evolution", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-1", "text": "7.3.1 Amplitude evolution § After \\(k\\) iterations the state has turned by \\(2k\\theta\\) from its starting angle \\(\\theta\\): \\[\\ket{\\psi_k}=\\sin\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\ket G+\\cos\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\ket B,\\qquad p_k=\\sin^2\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr).\\] Which marked label do we find? All marked labels share the amplitude of \\(\\ket G\\), so given success, the result is uniform over the marked set. The success probability depends on the marked fraction \\(M/N\\), not on which labels are marked. For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), \\(p_k=\\sin^2((2k+1)\\pi/6)\\). One iteration is ideal, but the next two return to probability \\(1/4\\). Stopping is part of the algorithm. Problems What formula gives success probability after \\(k\\) Grover iterations? With \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), evaluate \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta)\\) for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\). What does the oscillation of success probability tell you about the difference between coherent amplification and a process that irreversibly accumulates successful outcomes? Why need success not grow monotonically?"}, {"title": "Iteration count and overshooting", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-2", "text": "7.3.2 Iteration count and overshooting § How many iterations should we perform? Success is greatest when \\((2k+1)\\theta\\) is close to \\(\\pi/2\\), that is, for \\(k\\) close to \\(\\pi/(4\\theta)-1/2\\). For a small marked fraction \\(\\theta\\approx\\sqrt{M/N}\\), so the number of iterations is about \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\sqrt{N/M}\\): of order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\), where a classical search needs of order \\(N/M\\) queries. Since \\(k\\) must be an integer, success is usually close to \\(1\\) but not exactly \\(1\\); \\(N=4\\) with \\(M=1\\) is a special case in which it is exact. Does iterating longer help? No. For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), the success probabilities for \\(k=0,1,2\\) are \\(1/4\\), \\(1\\), and \\(1/4\\): the second iteration rotates the state past the good direction. The stopping rule is part of the algorithm . Predict first. Set Search space to 4 and reset. There is one marked item; start from equal amplitudes. Predict the success probability after one iteration and after a second. Does another iteration always help? Experiment: rotate toward the marked item There is one marked item. A full Grover iteration rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\), where \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt N\\). Keep stepping after the first peak to see why overshooting matters. Search space 4 16 64 256 One iteration Play Reset Iterations 0 unmarked component → marked component ↑ Probability of finding the marked item: 6.25%. The initial state spreads probability uniformly over all items. After trying it: compare your explanation One iteration gives success probability 1. A second gives \\(1/4\\): continued rotation can move the state away from the marked direction. Compare this with the initial probability \\(1/4\\). Problems Can another Grover iteration reduce success probability? For \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), compare success at \\(k=1\\) with success at \\(k=2\\) using the iteration formula. An iteration rule can increase success initially and decrease it later. What information is needed to choose a stopping point? Why is “keep improving until certain” not an adequate instruction here?"}, {"title": "Unknown marked fraction and verification", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-3", "text": "7.3.3 Unknown marked fraction and verification § What if we do not know \\(M\\), and so cannot compute the stopping point? A fixed guess may overshoot. A standard approach chooses a random number of iterations from a range that grows gradually, measures, and checks the candidate with one query to \\(f\\). When \\(M\\gt 0\\), such schedules still need an expected number of queries of order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\). What if nothing is marked? Then no check ever succeeds, and an unbounded search never ends; it cannot prove that nothing is marked. A bounded version stops after a fixed budget and reports failure, with a stated probability of being wrong when marked labels do exist. That is a statistical guarantee, not a proof of absence. If every label is marked, the very first measurement succeeds. Further detail: Boyer, Brassard, Høyer, and Tapp: Tight bounds on quantum searching . A failed verification rejects that candidate, not every possible candidate. With an unknown marked fraction, the schedule must choose how long to search before measuring again. Problems What does verification check after a candidate is measured? A measured candidate fails the predicate. Does that prove that every candidate fails? Explain with a four-item example. Why does verifying a candidate solve a different problem from knowing how many amplification steps to use? If a run fails verification, what has it established—and what has it not established—about the existence of a marked item?"}, {"title": "Arbitrary state preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-1", "text": "7.4.1 Arbitrary state preparation § Was the uniform start essential? Suppose a unitary \\(A\\) prepares \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and \\(P_G\\) projects onto the outcomes we count as successes, with success probability \\(p=\\|P_G\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\), where \\(0\\lt p\\lt 1\\). Normalizing the good and bad parts writes \\[\\ket\\psi=\\sqrt p\\,\\ket G+\\sqrt{1-p}\\,\\ket B.\\] This is the same plane as before, with \\(\\sin^2\\theta=p\\). Where might \\(A\\) come from? A classical randomized procedure can sometimes be made coherent and serve as \\(A\\), but only through an explicit reversible implementation, including its random bits and its workspace. Access to a routine that produces samples is not enough. A nonuniform preparation can still be resolved into normalized good and bad components. This example has \\(p=1/4\\), so its amplitudes are \\(1/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt3/2\\) regardless of how many underlying labels contribute. Problems Must amplitude amplification start from a uniform superposition? A preparation succeeds with probability \\(1/4\\). Identify its good and bad amplitude magnitudes using \\(\\sqrt p\\) and \\(\\sqrt{1-p}\\). If an initial preparation already favors good answers, how should that change amplification? Which feature of the preparation matters more than the raw number of basis states?"}, {"title": "Reflection using preparation and its inverse", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-2", "text": "7.4.2 Reflection using preparation and its inverse § The search reflected about \\(\\ket s\\). How do we reflect about \\(\\ket\\psi\\) without knowing it? Reflection about \\(\\ket0\\), \\(2\\ket0\\bra0-I\\), is easy: it negates every configuration except \\(0\\). Bring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) with \\(A^\\dagger\\), reflect there, and return with \\(A\\): \\[R_\\psi=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I=A\\bigl(2\\ket0\\bra0-I\\bigr)A^\\dagger.\\] Applied to a state, the rightmost factor \\(A^\\dagger\\) acts first. Together with \\(I-2P_G\\), which negates the successful part, \\(R_\\psi\\) rotates the state toward the good direction exactly as in search. What does this require? Coherent access to \\(A\\), to its inverse, and to the success test. Measuring whether a sample succeeded is no substitute: it destroys the superposition instead of reflecting it. The inverse preparation acts first. Conjugating the zero-state reflection produces reflection about the prepared state. Problems Which inverse appears in the reflection \\(A(2\\ketbra00-I)A^\\dagger\\)? Apply this reflection to \\(A\\ket0\\) by first using \\(A^\\dagger A=I\\). Does the prepared state change sign? Why does implementing reflection about a prepared state require access to the preparation’s inverse? Is being able to obtain a copy of that state by an uncontrolled process enough to build the same reflection?"}, {"title": "Success probability and resource cost", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-3", "text": "7.4.3 Success probability and resource cost § What do we gain? With independent trials, each succeeding with probability \\(p\\), we need about \\(1/p\\) preparations for a fixed chance of success. Amplitude amplification needs about \\(1/\\sqrt p\\) iterations, each using \\(A\\), \\(A^\\dagger\\), and the success test once. If \\(A\\) costs \\(C_A\\) and the test costs \\(C_G\\), the total is of order \\((C_A+C_G)/\\sqrt p\\), plus the cost of verifying the answer and of choosing the number of iterations. The improvement is quadratic in the number of uses of these resources. If preparing the state or building the oracle is itself expensive, that cost can dominate the total. For small \\(p\\), the improvement concerns repeated use of coherent preparation and the success test. Their implementation costs must be included. Problems What costs besides iteration count enter amplitude amplification? Suppose each round uses 12 gates and the initial preparation uses 8. Calculate the cost of five rounds, ignoring measurement. A better initial success probability can reduce the number of amplification rounds, yet its preparation may be expensive. How would you decide whether the improved preparation saves total work?"}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-1", "text": "Does applying a phase oracle immediately increase a marked outcome’s probability? No. The oracle changes a sign, not its squared magnitude. A subsequent reflection converts that phase difference into a change of amplitudes."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-2", "text": "For four equal amplitudes of \\(1/2\\) with one marked item, calculate one marking step and one reflection about the mean. The marked amplitude becomes \\(1/2-(-1/2)=1\\). Each unmarked amplitude becomes \\(1/2-1/2=0\\). Measuring returns the marked item with certainty after one ideal iteration."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-3", "text": "In that four-item example, what happens if you apply a second Grover iteration before measuring? The success probability falls to \\(\\sin^2(5\\pi/6)=1/4\\). More iterations need not improve the result: the state rotates past the good direction."}, {"title": "Wires and gate ordering", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-1", "text": "8.1.1 Wires and gate ordering § How can we write down a long sequence of operations on many qubits without writing out their matrices? Draw one horizontal line, a wire , for each qubit , with time running from left to right, and a box on a wire for each operation applied to that qubit. This is a circuit diagram . What does a wire carry? Not a definite bit. When the register is in a superposition or an entangled state, no single wire has a value of its own. To read a circuit, follow the joint state of the whole register from one column of gates to the next. In what order are the matrices multiplied? If \\(U\\) acts first and \\(V\\) second, the final state is \\(VU\\ket\\psi\\): the drawing reads from left to right, and the product from right to left. Does the order of gates on different wires matter? No. Gates on disjoint wires act as tensor products, such as \\(U\\otimes I\\) and \\(I\\otimes V\\), and these commute. A real device may still have to schedule them in a particular way. The circuit applies \\(H\\) first and \\(Z\\) second. On a column vector the rightmost matrix acts first, so the combined operator is \\(ZH\\). Problems In which direction does time run in the circuit drawings? A circuit applies \\(H\\) and then \\(Z\\). Write the matrix product acting on the input column vector in the correct order. Why can interchanging two adjacent gates change the result even when the set of gates is unchanged? Give a condition under which the interchange is safe and explain why wire labels matter."}, {"title": "Circuits for query and search algorithms", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-2", "text": "8.1.2 Circuits for query and search algorithms § Read the algorithms we have met as circuits. In Deutsch’s circuit, the first pair of \\(H\\) gates prepares \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), the oracle box puts the function into signs, and the last \\(H\\) exposes their relation. In search, a block consisting of the phase oracle and the reflection \\(D\\) is repeated. Does a box labelled \\(O_f\\) tell us how to build \\(O_f\\)? No. Drawing an operation as a single box does not make it a single elementary gate; its cost is unknown until we say how it is implemented. A query diagram counts uses of an oracle. Replacing each box by a circuit exposes the gate cost, plus any operations between calls. Problems What does an oracle box stand for in a query circuit? A circuit uses three oracle boxes, each requiring ten gates when expanded. Count the gates contributed by those boxes. A circuit diagram can represent an entire oracle by one box. What must be specified before you can infer an implementation cost from that diagram? Which conclusions can still be drawn while the box remains abstract?"}, {"title": "Measurement and classical control", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-3", "text": "8.1.3 Measurement and classical control § How does a circuit show a measurement? A meter symbol ends the quantum description of that wire and produces a classical bit. In our drawings, solid lines carry qubits and dashed lines carry classical bits. Is a gate controlled by a measured bit the same as a controlled gate? No. A classically controlled gate is chosen using a known result. A coherent controlled gate acts on an unmeasured control that may be in a superposition, and it can create entanglement . Teleportation uses both: a coherent CNOT before Alice’s measurement, and classically controlled corrections afterward. Classical control uses a recorded outcome. It selects a branch after measurement rather than preserving a coherent superposition of both control alternatives. Problems What kind of information controls a classically conditioned correction? A measurement produces bit \\(b\\), and \\(X\\) is applied only if \\(b=1\\). Write the operation performed for each possible \\(b\\). How does controlling an operation with a measured bit differ from controlling it coherently with a qubit? What happens to the possibility of interference between the control alternatives?"}, {"title": "Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-1", "text": "8.2.1 Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates § Which one-qubit gates do we need? We have met three: \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] A fourth, \\(Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\), completes the Pauli matrices \\(I\\), \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\). How do we turn a qubit by an arbitrary amount? For a Pauli matrix \\(P\\), define the rotation gate \\[R_P(\\theta)=\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\,I-i\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\,P.\\] Is it unitary ? Since \\(P^\\dagger=P\\) and \\(P^2=I\\), we get \\(R_P(\\theta)^\\dagger R_P(\\theta)=\\cos^2\\tfrac\\theta2\\,I+\\sin^2\\tfrac\\theta2\\,P^2=I\\). It is. Two fixed phase gates appear often: \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and \\(T=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\pi/4})\\). They change the relative phase of \\(\\ket1\\) by \\(\\pi/2\\) and by \\(\\pi/4\\). Predict first. Starting from \\(\\ket0\\), compare applying \\(H\\) twice with applying \\(H\\), then \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\). Predict both final measurement distributions before trying the gates. Experiment: a gate changes amplitudes Start in \\(\\ket{0}\\). Apply \\(H\\) twice, then compare \\(HZH\\) with a single \\(X\\). Orange marks a negative amplitude; the bar length shows its squared magnitude. Apply H Apply X Apply Z Reset to \\(\\ket0\\) State: \\(1\\ket0+0\\ket1\\) Choose a gate to change the state. This experiment uses real amplitudes. It illustrates X, Z, and H, not every possible qubit state. After trying it: compare your explanation \\(H\\) followed by \\(H\\) returns \\(\\ket0\\), so 0 is certain. \\(H\\), \\(Z\\), \\(H\\) produces \\(\\ket1\\), so 1 is certain. \\(Z\\) changes a relative sign that the final \\(H\\) converts into a different outcome. \\(X\\) exchanges basis labels; \\(Z\\) changes one sign; \\(H\\) changes basis. Their action on arbitrary superpositions follows by linearity. Problems What does the \\(X\\) gate do to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\)? Compare \\(Z\\ket0\\) with \\(Z\\ket+\\). Which input makes the relative-sign change apparent? Applying \\(Z\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) changes nothing observable, but placing \\(Z\\) between two Hadamards changes the final outcome. Why is testing a gate on one input insufficient to establish that the gate has no effect?"}, {"title": "Controlled and entangling gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-2", "text": "8.2.2 Controlled and entangling gates § How do we make a gate act on a target only when a control qubit is \\(1\\)? Use the projectors onto the two values of the control: \\[\\text{controlled-}U=\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I+\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes U.\\] It keeps the control and applies \\(U\\) to the target in the branch where the control is \\(1\\). CNOT is controlled-\\(X\\). Controlled-\\(Z\\) multiplies only \\(\\ket{11}\\) by \\(-1\\). Can one be made from the other? Since \\(HXH=Z\\), applying \\(H\\) to the target before and after a CNOT gives controlled-\\(Z\\). Do controlled gates always entangle? Not on every input: CNOT leaves \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) a product state . On \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\), however, it produces a Bell state . Hadamards on the target convert controlled-X to controlled-Z. Problems Which qubit changes in a CNOT operation? Compare CNOT on \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) with CNOT on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\). Write both outputs and identify which is entangled. A controlled gate is capable of entangling some inputs but leaves others as product states. What features of the control and target preparations decide which happens? Test CNOT on a basis control and on a superposed control."}, {"title": "Oracle and reflection circuits", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-3", "text": "8.2.3 Oracle and reflection circuits § How is a phase oracle built? To mark one bit string, flip with \\(X\\) each of its positions that is \\(0\\), apply a phase flip controlled on all qubits being \\(1\\), and undo the flips. For a predicate computed by a circuit, use compute, phase, uncompute. And the reflection \\(D\\)? Since \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) maps \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) to \\(\\ket s\\), we can reflect about \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) between two layers of Hadamards: \\[D=H^{\\otimes n}\\bigl(2\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n}-I\\bigr)H^{\\otimes n}.\\] The two-qubit drawing implements the reflection about \\(\\ket{00}\\) with \\(X\\) gates and a controlled-\\(Z\\), and so produces \\(-D\\). The sign is a global phase and changes nothing in an ordinary Grover iteration ; it would matter if the iteration were itself controlled. The oracle and reflection are decomposed here for one explicit predicate; a general predicate needs its own circuit. Problems Why are temporary workspace wires included in an oracle circuit? An implementation returns the correct answer but leaves a record \\(g(x)\\) in workspace. State the extra cleanup required before input branches can interfere without that record. When replacing an oracle box by a circuit, why must you check the final workspace as well as the output bits? Could a circuit have the correct truth table on its output and still spoil the intended quantum algorithm?"}, {"title": "Limits of local operations", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-1", "text": "8.3.1 Limits of local operations § Can one-qubit gates alone produce every operation? A circuit of one-qubit gates acts as \\(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\). On a product state it gives another product state: \\[(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n)(\\ket{\\psi_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\ket{\\psi_n})=U_1\\ket{\\psi_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\ket{\\psi_n}.\\] It can never prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\), however many gates it uses. The argument settles the question without counting anything: a universal set needs a gate that lets qubits affect one another. Applying \\(A\\) to one qubit and \\(B\\) to the other gives \\((A\\ket\\psi)\\otimes(B\\ket\\phi)\\). Starting with a product, local unitary gates alone cannot create entanglement. Problems Can local unitary gates create entanglement from a product state? Apply \\(H\\otimes I\\) to \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) and write the result as a product of two one-qubit states. Why can local unitary operations change the appearance of an entangled state without removing its entanglement? Use the inverse operations to test what would follow if they could turn it into a product state."}, {"title": "Entangling gates and universality", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-2", "text": "8.3.2 Entangling gates and universality § Is a single entangling gate enough? It is. One-qubit gates together with CNOT can build every unitary on any number of qubits; a set with this property is universal . One proof writes a unitary as a product of operations that each act on just two basis states, and builds each of these from controlled operations and one-qubit rotations. We state the theorem without the full proof. Does universality make every operation cheap? No. A general unitary on \\(n\\) qubits has a number of parameters exponential in \\(n\\), and most such unitaries need exponentially many gates. Algorithms are useful because their operations have structure that permits short circuits. Further detail: Barenco et al.: Elementary gates for quantum computation . Arbitrary one-qubit unitaries together with CNOT suffice to construct arbitrary finite-register unitaries. A chosen finite gate alphabet instead generally requires approximation. Problems What does a universal gate set allow us to construct or approximate? A collection contains only single-qubit gates. Starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\), can it prepare a Bell state? Use the product-state form to explain. Why does the availability of an entangling gate need to be considered together with the allowed one-qubit gates? What is missing from the claim that any collection containing one entangling operation is automatically universal?"}, {"title": "Exact synthesis and approximation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-3", "text": "8.3.3 Exact synthesis and approximation § Real hardware offers a finite set of gates. Can a finite set build every unitary exactly? No: there are only countably many finite circuits, but uncountably many unitaries. The best a finite set can do is approximate every unitary as closely as we wish. The Clifford+T set, built from \\(H\\), \\(S\\), CNOT, and \\(T\\), does this. How do we measure “close”? Use the operator-norm error \\(\\|U-V\\|\\leq\\epsilon\\): for every unit input, the two outputs differ by a vector of length at most \\(\\epsilon\\). What happens in a circuit of \\(L\\) approximated gates? The errors add at most: if gate \\(i\\) is within \\(\\epsilon_i\\), the whole circuit is within \\(\\sum_i\\epsilon_i\\). A total tolerance \\(\\epsilon\\) can therefore be met by allowing each gate \\(\\epsilon/L\\). A gate count means little without the accuracy it was computed for. The task is to find a finite circuit \\(V\\) close enough to a target \\(U\\). The tolerance controls acceptable error and affects the required circuit length. Problems What distinguishes exact synthesis from approximate synthesis? A circuit’s error is at most 0.01. Does this meet a requested tolerance of 0.1? Does it meet 0.001? Why does approximating every desired operation arbitrarily closely differ from expressing every operation exactly with finitely many gates? What additional question about approximation cost matters to computation?"}, {"title": "Gate count, depth, and workspace", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-1", "text": "8.4.1 Gate count, depth, and workspace § What does a circuit cost? Three measures matter: the gate count , the total number of elementary gates; the depth , the number of layers of gates that must run one after another; and the width , the number of qubits, including extra workspace qubits called ancillas . These trade off. More workspace can reduce depth, while recomputing an intermediate value instead of storing it can save qubits. Ancillas must start in a known state and are usually returned to it by uncomputation , so that they can be reused. There is a subtler cost. An algorithm needs a circuit for every input length \\(n\\). A circuit family is uniform if a classical procedure produces the circuit for each \\(n\\), and the family counts as efficient only if that procedure is efficient too. Otherwise a short circuit could hide hard work in its design. Four independent gates on four wires can occupy one layer. Four dependent gates on one wire require four layers. Parallelism may use more workspace. Problems What is the difference between gate count and circuit depth? Four one-qubit gates act on four different qubits with no dependencies. Find their gate count and minimum depth, assuming parallel execution is available. Two circuits use the same number of gates but have different dependencies. Why might their running times differ? What other resource could increase when you try to reduce their depth?"}, {"title": "Connectivity and data preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-2", "text": "8.4.2 Connectivity and data preparation § Can any two qubits interact? In a circuit diagram, yes; in a device, often only neighbouring qubits can. Gates between distant qubits then need SWAP gates to bring them together, which adds to the count and the depth. Is loading data free? Preparing an arbitrary list of amplitudes can take work proportional to the length of the list, even though the register holding it is small. Likewise, the arithmetic that implements a predicate can cost far more than the number of oracle calls suggests. A resource estimate must therefore state the gate set, the connectivity, how inputs are prepared, and how outputs are read, before an asymptotic advantage can become a claim about real machines. On a three-site line only neighboring sites interact directly. To act on logical qubits A and C, swap A next to C, apply the gate, and restore the placement if needed. Problems What does hardware connectivity specify? Three qubits lie in a line with interactions only between neighbors. Which pair cannot interact directly, and what kind of extra operation could bring its information together? A circuit assumes that two distant qubits can interact. How does restricted hardware connectivity change the operations actually required? Why should loading the input also be counted before claiming an end-to-end advantage?"}, {"title": "Error accumulation and reliability", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-3", "text": "8.4.3 Error accumulation and reliability § Real gates are imperfect. Small systematic errors and random noise accumulate over a long circuit. This physical noise differs from the approximation error of gate synthesis, although both change the predictions. Can errors be corrected? Quantum error correction encodes one logical qubit in correlations among several physical qubits, measures error syndromes that reveal which error occurred without revealing the encoded state, and then corrects the error. Does this contradict the no-cloning theorem? No: a code does not make independent copies of the state; it spreads the state into correlations. This book does not develop noise models or the threshold theorem. Reliability is a further resource requirement, beyond proving that an ideal algorithm is correct. For ideal and implemented unitary gates with operator-norm error at most ε per gate, a telescoping bound gives total error at most mε. The bound is not a claim that all errors add with the same sign in a particular run. Problems What does a per-gate error bound describe? Using the bound that errors add, estimate the total error bound for 100 gates each with error at most 0.0001. Why can a small error in each gate become important in a long circuit? What assumptions would justify adding error bounds, and why should that worst-case bound not be mistaken for a prediction of the actual error in every run?"}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-1", "text": "Do \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) and \\(Z\\) then \\(H\\) have the same action on \\(\\ket0\\)? No. \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) gives \\(\\ket-\\); \\(Z\\) then \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket+\\). Matrix multiplication order must match the circuit’s temporal order."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-2", "text": "Show that \\(H\\) on the target, CNOT, then \\(H\\) on the target implements controlled-\\(Z\\). For control 0, the target undergoes \\(H\\cdot I\\cdot H=I\\). For control 1, it undergoes \\(H\\cdot X\\cdot H=Z\\). Only \\(\\ket{11}\\) gains a minus sign, so the joint operation is controlled-\\(Z\\). Linearity extends the result to superpositions."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-3", "text": "A query algorithm uses \\(q\\) oracle calls. Each compiled oracle needs \\(g\\) elementary gates. What bound on total gates follows from \\(q\\) alone? No total gate bound follows from \\(q\\) alone. If each call costs \\(g\\) gates, the calls contribute \\(qg\\) gates, plus other operations. If \\(g\\) depends on input size or precision, it must remain in the analysis. Query savings alone do not establish a complete implementation advantage."}, {"title": "Input size and success criteria", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-1", "text": "9.1.1 Input size and success criteria § When can we say that one method is faster than another? Only when both solve the same task on the same terms: the same encoding of inputs, the same measure of input size, the same access to data, and the same standard of success. Why does the encoding matter? Size is measured by the length of the input. An integer \\(N\\) written in binary has about \\(\\log_2N\\) bits, so a procedure that takes \\(N\\) steps takes a number of steps exponential in the input length. Which cost do we report? The worst case over all inputs, the average over a stated distribution of inputs, or the expected cost of an algorithm that uses randomness. Each is legitimate, but they must not be mixed. In a query model, we must also say what one query returns. Changing the interface changes the problem, not merely the method used to solve it. Binary encoding uses logarithmically many bits. A cost proportional to the integer’s value can therefore be exponential in the number of input bits. Problems What is meant by input length for a binary integer? Write 8 in binary and count its bits. Compare the value 8 with the input length. An integer’s value can be exponentially larger than the number of bits used to write it. Why must an efficiency claim identify the encoding? How might the same running-time formula be classified differently for binary and unary inputs?"}, {"title": "Asymptotic notation", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-2", "text": "9.1.2 Asymptotic notation § Costs are functions of the input length \\(n\\). How do we compare their growth without tracking every constant? We write \\(T(n)=O(g(n))\\) if there is a constant \\(C\\gt 0\\) with \\(T(n)\\leq Cg(n)\\) for all large enough \\(n\\): an eventual upper bound. \\(\\Omega(g(n))\\) is the corresponding lower bound , and \\(\\Theta(g(n))\\) means both. For example, \\(3n^2+7n+2=\\Theta(n^2)\\): for \\(n\\geq1\\) it lies between \\(n^2\\) and \\(12n^2\\). What do these notations hide? Constant factors, and nothing else: not the encoding, and not the model. A polynomial bound \\(n^k\\) grows far more slowly than an exponential bound such as \\(2^n\\). Still, a polynomial with a large exponent can be impractical at the sizes that matter. The linear algorithm with a large constant eventually wins, but not for every input size. Big-O notation describes growth, not a complete timing prediction. Problems What does an \\(O(n^2)\\) upper bound say about growth? For \\(n\\ge1\\), bound \\(3n^2+2n\\) by a constant times \\(n^2\\). Give a suitable constant. Two algorithms have costs \\(n^2\\) and a million times \\(n\\). What does asymptotic comparison tell you, and what does it leave open for inputs you can actually run? Why are both questions worth asking?"}, {"title": "Query, gate, and space complexity", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-3", "text": "9.1.3 Query, gate, and space complexity § Which resource are we counting? Query complexity counts oracle uses, gate complexity counts elementary operations, and space complexity counts memory. Physical running time depends further on scheduling, error correction, and the device. Does an advantage in one resource carry over to another? Not automatically. A quantum query advantage may survive, or disappear, once the cost of implementing each query and of preparing the input is counted. For unstructured search, the quadratic reduction in queries is a theorem. It is not a theorem that searching any stored dataset on any quantum device is quadratically faster overall. These totals count only the gates inside query calls. Preparation and other gates must still be added for a full comparison. Problems What does query complexity count? Algorithm A uses 5 queries of 100 gates each; B uses 10 queries of 10 gates each. Compare their query counts and the gates used by their queries. How could one algorithm use fewer queries but more elementary gates than another? Why is there no meaningful claim of “fewer resources” until the resource being compared is named?"}, {"title": "P, NP, and reductions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-1", "text": "9.2.1 P, NP, and reductions § Which problems count as efficiently solvable? Consider decision problems , whose answer is yes or no. P is the class of decision problems that a deterministic algorithm solves in time polynomial in the input length. Some problems seem harder to solve than to check. NP is the class of decision problems whose yes instances have a witness , a certificate of polynomial size that can be checked in polynomial time. Equivalently, a nondeterministic machine accepts when some possible run guesses a witness and checks it. This is not a claim that a random guess will probably succeed. How do we compare the difficulty of two problems? A polynomial-time reduction from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) converts each instance of \\(A\\) into an instance of \\(B\\) with the same answer, so a fast solver for \\(B\\) gives a fast solver for \\(A\\). Whether P equals NP, that is, whether every efficiently checkable problem is efficiently solvable, is unknown. If task \\(A\\) reduces efficiently to task \\(B\\), an efficient solver for \\(B\\) would yield one for \\(A\\). The arrow describes converting instances, not proving that such a solver exists. Problems In the definition of NP, what is checked efficiently? A proposed factor \\(d\\) of \\(N\\) is supplied. Describe how division checks this proposal without finding \\(d\\) yourself. Checking a proposed solution efficiently is different from finding one efficiently. What would an efficient reduction allow you to transfer between problems, and why would that still not by itself settle whether P equals NP?"}, {"title": "BPP and BQP", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-2", "text": "9.2.2 BPP and BQP § What if an algorithm may use randomness and occasionally err? BPP is the class of decision problems solved in polynomial time by a randomized classical algorithm whose probability of error is at most \\(1/3\\) on every input. Why \\(1/3\\)? Any constant below \\(1/2\\) would do: repeating the algorithm and taking the majority answer drives the error down quickly. BQP is the quantum counterpart: decision problems solved, with error at most \\(1/3\\), by a uniform family of polynomial-size quantum circuits. How do the classes compare? A quantum circuit can simulate a randomized classical computation, so \\(\\mathrm{BPP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BQP}\\). Is BQP larger? This is not known, and neither is whether BQP contains NP. These are classes of decision problems; factoring is usually stated as a task whose answer is a number. Both classes concern polynomial-time decision procedures with a bounded error on every allowed input. The computational model differs. Problems Which model of computation distinguishes BPP from BQP? An algorithm must succeed with probability at least \\(2/3\\) on every input. Does success on 99 out of 100 tested inputs establish that guarantee for all inputs? Explain. Why must the error guarantee hold for every allowed input rather than only on average over a convenient test set? What would experiments on a small collection of instances fail to establish about membership in BPP or BQP?"}, {"title": "Factoring and search", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-3", "text": "9.2.3 Factoring and search § What evidence is there that quantum computers are more powerful? There are two kinds, of different strength. Factoring: quantum period finding gives a factoring algorithm whose running time is polynomial in the number of digits; its derivation lies beyond this book. No polynomial-time classical algorithm is known. No one has proved that none exists, however, so this is an advantage over known methods, not a proven separation. Search: Grover’s algorithm has a proved quadratic advantage, but only in the unstructured query model. Neither kind of evidence suggests that quantum computers speed up every task. Factoring uses arithmetic structure; unstructured search assumes only oracle access to a predicate. A lower bound for the latter is not a lower bound for every structured problem. Problems Is unstructured search assumed to provide useful relations between different candidates? Compare factoring an integer with searching an arbitrary list. Identify the arithmetic structure available in the first task. Why can an efficient quantum factoring algorithm and a quadratic quantum search improvement coexist without contradiction? Which structural difference prevents an unstructured-search lower bound from automatically applying to factoring?"}, {"title": "Upper and lower bounds", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-1", "text": "9.3.1 Upper and lower bounds § How do we know a problem cannot be solved faster? An upper bound needs only one algorithm and an analysis of its cost. A lower bound must hold for every algorithm the model allows, including ones that nobody has invented. A slow known method proves nothing about faster unknown ones. Why are query models useful here? In a query model, an algorithm may do anything it likes between queries, but the hidden input affects it only through the queries it makes. That restriction is what makes lower-bound arguments possible. A query lower bound is strong within the model, though it says nothing by itself about the cost of each query. A construction proves that a task can be solved within a cost. A lower bound must cover every permitted algorithm in the stated model. Problems Does an upper bound describe one available method or rule out every faster method? Someone gives an \\(O(n^2)\\) algorithm and later someone gives an \\(O(n)\\) algorithm. Which upper bounds are still valid? Why does inventing a faster algorithm refute a proposed lower bound but never refute a valid upper bound? What different burdens of proof do these two kinds of claim carry?"}, {"title": "Unstructured search optimality", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-2", "text": "9.3.2 Unstructured search optimality § Can a quantum algorithm search faster than Grover’s? No. Finding a unique marked item among \\(N\\) with constant success probability requires \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) queries. Grover’s algorithm uses \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\), so the query complexity of the task is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). Why can it not be faster? A single query can change the state substantially only through the amplitude on the marked label, and averaged over all possible positions of the mark, that amplitude is small. The hybrid argument makes this precise; we state its conclusion as a theorem. The geometry of the Grover iteration proves an upper bound, not optimality. For one marked item and constant success in the unstructured quantum query model, Grover’s \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) upper bound and the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound give \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) query complexity. Problems What resource does the standard unstructured-search lower bound count? If extra structure lets a classical algorithm avoid inspecting unrelated items, is it still the same unstructured access model? Explain. An optimality theorem rules out faster unstructured search in a particular query model. Which part of the problem would have to change for a faster method to be possible without contradicting that theorem?"}, {"title": "Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional)", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-3", "text": "9.3.3 Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional) § Optional derivation · polynomial lower bounds Is there another route to lower bounds? Write the hidden input of the oracle as bits \\(z_1,\\dots,z_N\\). After \\(T\\) queries, every amplitude is a polynomial of degree at most \\(T\\) in these bits, and every acceptance probability is a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\). An algorithm that is correct with probability at least \\(2/3\\) therefore yields a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\) that approximates the function it computes on every Boolean input. If every such approximating polynomial needs degree at least \\(d\\), the approximate degree , then \\(2T\\geq d\\), so \\(T\\geq d/2\\). For OR on \\(N\\) bits, which detects whether any item is marked, the approximate degree is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). This gives a second proof of the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound. Appendix E explains why the degree grows by at most one per query; the approximate degree of OR is a separate theorem. Further detail: Beals et al.: Quantum Lower Bounds by Polynomials . The query model limits acceptance degree to \\(2T\\). If approximating the requested function requires degree \\(d\\), then \\(2T\\ge d\\). The approximate-degree theorem is a separate ingredient. Problems After \\(T\\) queries, what is the maximum degree bound on an acceptance probability? A function has approximate degree \\(d=10\\). Use \\(T\\ge d/2\\) to obtain a query lower bound. Why is a low-degree polynomial representation useful for proving a lower bound only when paired with a theorem about the function being approximated? Which half of that argument is supplied by the query model, and which is a separate mathematical result?"}, {"title": "Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-1", "text": "9.4.1 Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions § What do the algorithms of this book have in common? Each uses coherent operations to make the property we want affect the probabilities of the final outcome. Deutsch’s algorithm turns the equality of two function values into a relative phase , and then into a definite bit. Amplitude amplification rotates a prepared state toward the successful outcomes. What do their advantages depend on? Specified oracles, coherent access to inverses where they are used, and a measurement chosen to expose the answer. The large state space gives room for these constructions, but size alone explains nothing: correctness is established by calculating the evolution and the readout. An advantage claim must include the access assumptions and success criterion. Expensive preparation or readout can dominate a reduction in oracle calls. Problems Name two assumptions that should accompany a claimed quantum speedup. An algorithm makes fewer queries but requires costly input preparation. Which additional cost must be included before comparing full implementations? If an algorithm’s speedup relies on inexpensive state preparation and oracle access, what happens when those assumptions fail? How would you separate a correct conditional advantage from an unsupported claim about a practical implementation?"}, {"title": "Computability and readout limitations", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-2", "text": "9.4.2 Computability and readout limitations § What can quantum computers not do? They do not escape the halting problem : a classical computer can simulate a quantum one, slowly, so both compute the same functions. They cannot copy unknown states, and a single measurement does not reveal all the amplitudes. Nor do they remove the cost of encoding inputs, building operations, or protecting states from noise. These constraints are part of the model, not footnotes added afterward. A useful claim of advantage names the task, the mechanism, the success guarantee, and the resources counted. The care that separates a pattern from a proof in mathematics also separates a plausible speedup from an established one. A faster implementation does not turn an undecidable task into a decidable one, nor make all amplitudes available from a single measurement. Problems What is the difference between computability and efficiency? A computer runs every step a million times faster. Does a program that never halts now halt? Explain. Why would a dramatic speedup on every problem a quantum computer can solve still not provide a general halting decider? What distinction between efficiency and computability survives any such improvement?"}, {"title": "Period finding and error correction: further topics", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-3", "text": "9.4.3 Period finding and error correction: further topics § Where does the subject go next? Period finding uses interference in a new way: a quantum Fourier transform turns periodic structure in a function into frequencies that a measurement reveals. Quantum error correction protects long computations by encoding states in subspaces and measuring syndromes. Both build on the language developed here, of states, operators, measurements, and circuits, but they need further constructions and proofs. They continue the present algorithms rather than underlie them. Whatever the algorithm, the same three things must be shown: a mechanism, a correct readout, and an honest resource bound. Period finding concerns making structure observable; error correction concerns protecting a computation from faults. A useful large computation needs both suitable algorithms and sufficiently reliable operations. Problems Which further topic protects computations against noise? Match “extract periodic structure” and “preserve encoded information despite errors” to period finding and error correction. Period finding concerns extracting structure; error correction concerns preserving a computation despite noise. Why does progress on either problem not automatically solve the other? What must a useful large computation achieve on both fronts?"}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-1", "text": "An algorithm uses \\(O(N)\\) steps on an integer \\(N\\) written in binary. Is that polynomial in input length? Not in general. The input length is of order \\(\\log_2 N\\). A number of steps proportional to \\(N\\) can grow exponentially with that length."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-2", "text": "One algorithm uses at most \\(3n^2+7n+2\\) operations. What asymptotic bound does this give, and does it prove that every algorithm needs quadratic time? It gives an \\(O(n^2)\\) upper bound for this algorithm. If the displayed expression is its actual cost, that cost is \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\). Neither statement proves a lower bound for all algorithms solving the task."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-3", "text": "Suppose a quantum search uses \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) queries, but each query requires \\(\\Theta(N)\\) gates in a chosen implementation. What do the query and gate counts say? Those calls contribute \\(\\Theta(N^{3/2})\\) gates to that implementation, before other costs. The quadratic query improvement remains a statement about oracle access. It does not show that this implementation beats a classical end-to-end method, nor rule out a better oracle implementation."}, {"title": "Automata and Memory", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-a", "text": "A. Automata and Memory How much memory does a computation need? Start with the least: a finite automaton , which reads its input once while keeping one of finitely many control states. Two states suffice to track parity, whether the number of ones read so far is even or odd: reading a one swaps the states, and reading a zero leaves them unchanged. Can a finite automaton check that a string consists of some number of \\(a\\)’s followed by the same number of \\(b\\)’s, the language \\(\\{a^nb^n:n\\geq0\\}\\)? No. A fixed automaton has finitely many states, so two different long prefixes \\(a^i\\) and \\(a^j\\) must reach the same state. Appending \\(b^i\\) then forces the same decision on \\(a^ib^i\\), which is in the language, and on \\(a^jb^i\\), which is not. This state-collision argument proves the limitation. What extra memory is enough? A stack , which can hold arbitrarily many pending symbols: push once for each \\(a\\), pop once for each \\(b\\), and accept when the input and the stack run out together. A pushdown automaton formalizes this memory. A linear-bounded automaton has a writable tape no longer than a constant multiple of the input, and a Turing machine removes even that bound. Do these models form a sequence of ever better machines? Not in that sense. More memory changes which configurations are available, not what a single step means. The models describe different classes of languages; they are not successive generations of hardware. Reading \\(aabb\\) pushes for each \\(a\\) and pops for each \\(b\\). The empty stack at the end certifies matching counts in this example; checking order is also required. Problems What kind of memory does a pushdown automaton add to a finite automaton? Process \\(aabb\\) by pushing once for each \\(a\\) and popping once for each \\(b\\). State the stack size after each symbol. Why does a collision between the states reached by two different prefixes prove a memory limitation? What continuation exposes that collision, and why does the argument depend on allowing arbitrarily long inputs?"}, {"title": "Grammars and Lambda Calculus", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-b", "text": "B. Grammars and Lambda Calculus Instead of recognizing strings, can we describe a set of strings by how its members are produced? A grammar does this. It specifies a start symbol, terminal symbols, nonterminal symbols, and replacement rules. The rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\), where \\(\\epsilon\\) is the empty string, generate exactly the strings of matching \\(a\\)’s and \\(b\\)’s: applying the first rule twice and then the second gives \\(aabb\\). How do grammars relate to machines? Generation asks what can be produced; recognition asks whether a given string belongs to the generated set. Regular grammars correspond to finite automata, context-free grammars to pushdown automata, context-sensitive grammars to linear-bounded automata, and unrestricted grammars to the languages a Turing machine recognizes, with the usual conventions about the empty string. Can computation be described without any machine at all? Lambda calculus uses only functions, application, and substitution. The reduction \\((\\lambda x.t)u\\to t[x:=u]\\) replaces the free occurrences of \\(x\\) in \\(t\\) by \\(u\\), renaming bound variables where necessary to avoid capture; for example, \\((\\lambda x.x)u\\to u\\). How can functions alone represent numbers? A Church numeral \\(n\\) is the function that applies a given function \\(n\\) times to a given starting value, so three is \\(\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f(f(x)))\\). Functions alone can encode data and iteration. The functions computable in lambda calculus are exactly the Turing-computable ones. That equivalence between formal models supports the broader Church–Turing thesis; it does not prove it. Apply \\(S\\to aSb\\) twice, then \\(S\\to\\) empty string. This demonstrates generation of \\(aabb\\); it does not yet give a procedure for rejecting arbitrary strings. Problems What string results from applying \\(S\\to aSb\\) once and then \\(S\\to\\) empty string? Generate \\(aabb\\) from \\(S\\) using the two rules. Write each intermediate string. Why does being able to generate every valid string not automatically give a procedure that rejects every invalid string? How does this distinction connect grammars back to proof search and decision?"}, {"title": "Linear Algebra Reference", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-c", "text": "C. Linear Algebra Reference This appendix collects the linear algebra used in the book; each identity is explained where it is first needed. How are complex vectors compared? Through the inner product \\(\\braket uv=u^\\dagger v\\), where \\(u^\\dagger=(u^*)^T\\) is the conjugate transpose. The norm is \\(\\sqrt{u^\\dagger u}\\). An orthonormal basis matrix \\(B\\) satisfies \\(B^\\dagger B=I\\), and \\(B^\\dagger\\) converts old coordinates to new ones. In which order do matrices act? From right to left on column vectors, and the adjoint reverses the order of a product: \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). Three properties of a matrix are easily confused. A unitary preserves overlaps; a Hermitian matrix equals its adjoint; an orthogonal projector satisfies \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\). How do tensor products behave? \\((A\\otimes B)(u\\otimes v)=Au\\otimes Bv\\) and \\(\\braket{u\\otimes v}{x\\otimes y}=\\braket ux\\braket vy\\). In the binary ordering used in this book, the leftmost qubit is the most significant label. How large can a matrix make a vector? The operator norm \\(\\|A\\|=\\max_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Av\\|\\) answers this. It gives uniform error bounds, because \\(\\|(U-V)v\\|\\leq\\|U-V\\|\\) for every unit vector \\(v\\). For the complex vector \\((3,4i)\\), the squared norm is \\(9+16=25\\). Dividing by five gives a unit vector; the imaginary coordinate contributes positively to its norm. Problems How do you find the norm of a complex vector? Calculate the norm of \\((3,4i)^T\\), then normalize the vector. A matrix is a representation, while a transformation acts on vectors independently of coordinates. Which identities in this reference ensure that changing coordinates consistently preserves lengths, overlaps, and predictions?"}, {"title": "Mixed States and General Measurements", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-d", "text": "D. Mixed States and General Measurements What if we know only that a system was prepared in one of several states, \\(\\ket{\\psi_r}\\) with probability \\(q_r\\)? No single state vector describes it. The density matrix \\[\\rho=\\sum_rq_r\\ket{\\psi_r}\\bra{\\psi_r}\\] does. It is positive semidefinite and has trace one, and a pure state has \\(\\rho=\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi\\). Different ensembles can give the same density matrix, and then they give the same predictions for every measurement. The measurement rule becomes \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\), and unitary evolution sends \\(\\rho\\) to \\(U\\rho U^\\dagger\\). What is the state of one part of an entangled pair? Its reduced state \\(\\rho_A=\\operatorname{tr}_B\\rho_{AB}\\), obtained by summing the diagonal blocks for \\(B\\) in a product basis. For either half of a Bell state , it is \\(I/2\\). Can an operation on \\(B\\) change \\(\\rho_A\\)? Not when its outcome is ignored. A trace-preserving operation on \\(B\\), with operators \\(M_r\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_rM_r^\\dagger M_r=I\\), leaves \\(\\rho_A\\) unchanged; inserting the sum into the partial trace shows this. Conditioning on the outcome \\(r\\) changes the description only for someone who knows \\(r\\). Are projective measurements the only ones? No. A general measurement has positive operators \\(E_j\\), called effects , that sum to the identity, with probabilities \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(E_j\\rho)\\). The effects fix the probabilities but not the state after the measurement; measurement operators supply that further information. What can a general measurement do that a projective one cannot? Suppose the state is either \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket+\\). With \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\), take \\[E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-,\\qquad E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1,\\qquad E_?=I-E_0-E_+.\\] The outcome \\(0\\) is impossible for \\(\\ket+\\), the outcome \\(+\\) is impossible for \\(\\ket0\\), and the outcome \\(?\\) is inconclusive. \\(E_?\\) is positive because the largest eigenvalue of \\(\\ket-\\bra-+\\ket1\\bra1\\) is \\(1+1/\\sqrt2\\). Whenever this measurement gives an answer, the answer is certain; the price is that it sometimes declines to answer. An equal mixture of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) and an equal mixture of \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) both yield \\(I/2\\). No measurement on the qubit alone identifies which ensemble was used. Problems What two properties must a density matrix satisfy? Form the density matrix for an equal mixture of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\). Check its trace and computational-basis probabilities. Two different ensembles can produce the same density matrix. What could any measurement on the prepared system reveal about which ensemble was used? How would access to a separate preparation record change the question?"}, {"title": "Polynomial Lower Bounds", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-e", "text": "E. Polynomial Lower Bounds Why must a search algorithm make many queries? In the Boolean query model, the hidden input is a string \\(z\\in\\{0,1\\}^N\\), and a query transforms \\(\\ket{i,b}\\) into \\(\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\). How does each query change the amplitudes? Every new amplitude is a combination of previous amplitudes multiplied by \\(z_i\\) or by \\(1-z_i\\). The amplitudes therefore start as constants and gain degree at most one per query. Operations that do not depend on the input only form linear combinations, which do not raise the degree. By induction, after \\(T\\) queries every amplitude is a polynomial of degree at most \\(T\\) in the input bits. What about the probability of accepting? It is a sum of squared magnitudes of amplitudes, so its degree is at most \\(2T\\). On Boolean inputs, any power \\(z_i^k\\) with \\(k\\geq1\\) equals \\(z_i\\), so the polynomial can be taken to be multilinear. The approximate degree of a Boolean function is the least degree of a real polynomial that approximates it, within a fixed error below one half, on every Boolean input. An algorithm with bounded error yields such a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\), so its number of queries is at least half the approximate degree. Proving that OR has approximate degree of order \\(\\sqrt N\\) needs an argument from approximation theory, beyond this short derivation. Together with the polynomial representation, that theorem gives the lower bound for detecting a marked item. Neither follows from the observation that known algorithms are slow. Starting from amplitudes independent of hidden input bits, each Boolean query raises their degree by at most one. Squaring magnitudes gives the factor of two for acceptance probabilities. Problems How much can one query increase the degree bound on an amplitude? For \\(T=3\\) queries, give the degree bounds on amplitudes and acceptance probabilities. Explain why the latter can be twice as large. Why do input-independent gates fail to increase polynomial degree while oracle queries can increase it? Which assumption about access to the hidden input would fail if the starting amplitudes already depended on its bits?"}, {"title": "Adjoint", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#adjoint", "text": "The conjugate transpose of a matrix, written with a dagger. It is not generally the inverse."}, {"title": "Algorithm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#algorithm", "text": "A finite set of precise instructions for carrying out a task. Whether it terminates on every allowed input must be established."}, {"title": "Amplitude", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude", "text": "A complex coefficient used to describe a quantum state or transition. The squared magnitude gives the corresponding probability in the specified measurement basis."}, {"title": "Amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude-amplification", "text": "A sequence of reflections that increases the amplitude in a designated successful subspace, given suitable preparation and inverse operations."}, {"title": "Bell state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bell-state", "text": "One of four maximally entangled two-qubit basis states. The book begins with the equal superposition of 00 and 11."}, {"title": "Boolean function", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#boolean-function", "text": "A function whose inputs and outputs are bits. A truth table specifies its value for every input."}, {"title": "BQP", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bqp", "text": "The class of decision problems solvable by uniform polynomial-size quantum circuits with bounded error."}, {"title": "Circuit depth", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#circuit-depth", "text": "The number of sequential layers of operations when compatible operations can run in parallel."}, {"title": "Computability", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#computability", "text": "Whether a task can be performed by an effective procedure, without imposing an efficiency requirement."}, {"title": "Consistency", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#consistency", "text": "A formal theory is consistent if its rules cannot prove both a statement and its negation."}, {"title": "Decision problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#decision-problem", "text": "A problem whose required answer is yes or no for each allowed input."}, {"title": "Entanglement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#entanglement", "text": "For a pure joint state, the inability to write it as a product of subsystem states. Mixed-state entanglement requires a broader definition."}, {"title": "Global phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#global-phase", "text": "A common phase factor multiplying an entire state vector. It leaves all measurement probabilities unchanged."}, {"title": "Grover iteration", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#grover-iteration", "text": "A phase reversal on marked items followed by a reflection about the prepared uniform state in the standard search algorithm."}, {"title": "Halting problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#halting-problem", "text": "The task of deciding, for every program and input, whether the program eventually stops. No algorithm solves it in full generality."}, {"title": "Hermitian", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hermitian", "text": "An operator equal to its adjoint. Its eigenvalues are real and its eigenspaces can describe a projective measurement."}, {"title": "Inner product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#inner-product", "text": "A scalar obtained from two vectors by conjugating the coordinates of the first and summing their products with the second. It measures their overlap."}, {"title": "Interference", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#interference", "text": "The addition of amplitudes for indistinguishable alternatives, which can increase or decrease an outcome probability."}, {"title": "Lower bound", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#lower-bound", "text": "A limit on the resources required by every algorithm within a specified model, rather than the cost of one construction."}, {"title": "NAND", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#nand", "text": "The Boolean operation that returns zero exactly when both input bits are one. NAND gates suffice to build every Boolean function."}, {"title": "Normalization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#normalization", "text": "The requirement that a state vector has squared norm one, so the probabilities of a complete basis measurement sum to one."}, {"title": "Oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#oracle", "text": "A specified operation that provides access to a function. Counting its uses does not by itself account for the cost of building it."}, {"title": "Phase kickback", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-kickback", "text": "When a controlled operation acts on a target eigenstate, its eigenvalue appears as a phase on the corresponding control component."}, {"title": "Product state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#product-state", "text": "A pure joint state that can be written as a tensor product of one state for each subsystem."}, {"title": "Projective measurement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#projective-measurement", "text": "A measurement described by mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity. Each outcome probability is the squared norm of the projected state."}, {"title": "Quantum advantage", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-advantage", "text": "An improvement over classical computation for a specified task, resource measure, and success guarantee. Its assumptions must be stated."}, {"title": "Quantum circuit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-circuit", "text": "An ordered description of operations on quantum registers, with any measurements and classical control specified."}, {"title": "Qubit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#qubit", "text": "A two-state quantum system. Its state is described by two amplitudes, one for each of the labels zero and one."}, {"title": "Query complexity", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#query-complexity", "text": "The number of oracle calls required for a task under a stated success criterion and oracle model."}, {"title": "Relative phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#relative-phase", "text": "The phase difference between two components of a state. It can affect probabilities after further operations."}, {"title": "Reversible", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#reversible", "text": "A transformation is reversible when its output determines its input uniquely on the allowed state space."}, {"title": "Superposition", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#superposition", "text": "A linear combination of basis states. Its components depend on the chosen basis; measurement does not reveal all coefficients at once."}, {"title": "Tensor product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#tensor-product", "text": "The construction of a joint vector space from component spaces. Basis labels combine in pairs and dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Transition weight", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#transition-weight", "text": "The number attached to an arrow from one configuration to another: 1 for a deterministic or allowed arrow, a probability for a probabilistic rule, a complex amplitude for a quantum operation."}, {"title": "Turing machine", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#turing-machine", "text": "A mathematical model with a tape, a read/write head, and finitely many control states. A transition rule specifies each step."}, {"title": "Uncomputation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#uncomputation", "text": "Applying the inverse of a computation to clear temporary workspace while retaining the needed result or phase."}, {"title": "Unitary", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#unitary", "text": "An operator whose adjoint is its inverse. Unitary transformations preserve inner products and describe closed-system quantum evolution."}, {"title": "Universal gate set", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#universal-gate-set", "text": "A set of gates sufficient to construct arbitrary quantum operations in the stated model, exactly or to a specified approximation."}, {"title": "Notation: not P", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-not-p", "text": "¬ P The negation of the sentence \\(P\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: f from n-bit strings to bits", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-f-from-n-bit-strings-to-bits", "text": "f:{0,1}ⁿ→{0,1} A Boolean function: one yes/no answer for each \\(n\\)-bit input string."}, {"title": "Notation: a XOR b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-xor-b", "text": "a⊕ b Exclusive OR: one when exactly one input is one. It is addition modulo two."}, {"title": "Notation: a and b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-and-b", "text": "a∧ b AND of two bits: one only when both are one."}, {"title": "Notation: k B T log two", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-k-b-t-log-two", "text": "k_BTln 2 Landauer’s minimum heat for erasing one equally likely bit at temperature \\(T\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: x maps to y", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-x-maps-to-y", "text": "x↦ y The rule that sends each input \\(x\\) to the output \\(y\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: U sub f", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-u-sub-f", "text": "U_f The reversible oracle for \\(f\\): it keeps \\(x\\) and XORs \\(f(x)\\) into a target, \\(U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,y\\oplus f(x)}\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: sum over j", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-sum-over-j", "text": "Σⱼ Add the following term for every value of \\(j\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: probability of j given i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-probability-of-j-given-i", "text": "P(j| i) Conditional probability: the chance of next state \\(j\\) when the current state is \\(i\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: r e to the i phi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-r-e-to-the-i-phi", "text": "re^(iφ) An amplitude with magnitude \\(r\\ge0\\) and phase \\(\\phi\\). Its outcome probability is \\(r^2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-i", "text": "i The imaginary unit, with \\(i^2=-1\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: z star", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-z-star", "text": "z* The complex conjugate: for \\(z=a+ib\\), \\(z^*=a-ib\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: magnitude of z", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-magnitude-of-z", "text": "|z| The size of a complex number. \\(|z|^2=z^*z\\) turns an amplitude into a probability weight."}, {"title": "Notation: v transpose", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-v-transpose", "text": "vᵀ Turns a row into a column or a column into a row; \\((a,b)^T\\) is a column vector."}, {"title": "Notation: ket psi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-psi", "text": "|ψ⟩ The column vector of a state, named \\(\\psi\\). The brackets name a vector; they are not an operation."}, {"title": "Notation: ket zero, ket one", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-zero-ket-one", "text": "|0⟩, |1⟩ The computational basis: \\(\\ket0=(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\ket1=(0,1)^T\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra phi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-phi", "text": "⟨φ| The conjugate-transpose row of \\(\\ket\\phi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra phi ket psi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-phi-ket-psi", "text": "⟨φ|ψ⟩ The complex inner product, or overlap, \\(\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket plus, ket minus", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-plus-ket-minus", "text": "|±⟩ The plus/minus basis, \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: norm of v", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-norm-of-v", "text": "|v| The length of a vector, \\(\\sqrt{\\braket vv}\\). A state vector has norm one."}, {"title": "Notation: ket a tensor ket b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-a-tensor-ket-b", "text": "|a⟩⊗|b⟩ The joint state of two systems, also written \\(\\ket{ab}\\). Dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Notation: Bell state phi plus", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bell-state-phi-plus", "text": "|Φ⁺⟩ The entangled state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket zero to the n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-zero-to-the-n", "text": "|0ⁿ⟩ All \\(n\\) qubits in state zero."}, {"title": "Notation: identity", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-identity", "text": "I The matrix that leaves every vector unchanged."}, {"title": "Notation: A dagger", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-dagger", "text": "A† The adjoint: the conjugate transpose \\((A^*)^T\\), defined by \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: diagonal a, b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-diagonal-a-b", "text": "diag(a,b) The diagonal matrix with entries \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket e bra e", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-e-bra-e", "text": "|e⟩⟨e| The projector onto the unit vector \\(\\ket e\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: P sub j", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-p-sub-j", "text": "Pⱼ The projector for measurement outcome \\(j\\). The Born rule gives \\(p(j)=\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: lambda", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-lambda", "text": "λ An eigenvalue: \\(A\\ket v=\\lambda\\ket v\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: X, Z, H", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-x-z-h", "text": "X, Z, H The bit flip, phase flip, and Hadamard gates."}, {"title": "Notation: A tensor n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-tensor-n", "text": "A^(⊗ n) \\(n\\) copies combined by the tensor product. \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) applies \\(H\\) to each of \\(n\\) qubits; \\(\\ket\\psi^{\\otimes2}\\) is two copies of \\(\\ket\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: rho", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-rho", "text": "ρ A density matrix, describing a preparation that need not be pure."}, {"title": "Notation: trace", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-trace", "text": "tr The sum of diagonal entries. With \\(\\rho\\), the Born rule is \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: minus one to the f of x", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-minus-one-to-the-f-of-x", "text": "(−1)^(f(x)) The sign a phase oracle attaches to input \\(x\\): \\(+1\\) when \\(f(x)=0\\), \\(-1\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: sine squared theta equals M over N", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-sine-squared-theta-equals-m-over-n", "text": "sin²θ=M/N Defines the search angle \\(\\theta\\) for \\(M\\) marked items among \\(N\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: big O of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-o-of-g-of-n", "text": "O(g(n)) An eventual upper bound up to a constant factor."}, {"title": "Notation: big omega of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-omega-of-g-of-n", "text": "Ω(g(n)) An eventual lower bound up to a constant factor."}, {"title": "Notation: big theta of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-theta-of-g-of-n", "text": "Θ(g(n)) Both an upper and a lower bound: growth exactly like \\(g(n)\\) up to constants."}, {"title": "Notation: BPP is contained in BQP", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bpp-is-contained-in-bqp", "text": "BPP⊆BQP Every problem in the first class is also in the second."}, {"title": "Notation: lambda x dot t, applied to u", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-lambda-x-dot-t-applied-to-u", "text": "(λ x.t)u In lambda calculus, a function with parameter \\(x\\) and body \\(t\\), applied to \\(u\\)."}];
